WEBVTT

1
00:01:09.862 --> 00:01:13.616
<i>In my younger
and more vulnerable years...</i>

2
00:01:13.825 --> 00:01:16.744
<i>...my father gave me some advice.</i>

3
00:01:19.205 --> 00:01:23.209
<i>"Always try to see the best in people,"
he would say.</i>

4
00:01:24.919 --> 00:01:29.841
<i>As a consequence,
I'm inclined to reserve all judgments.</i>

5
00:01:31.384 --> 00:01:35.430
<i>But even I have a limit.</i>

6
00:01:37.599 --> 00:01:42.186
<i>Back then, all of us drank too much.</i>

7
00:01:43.021 --> 00:01:46.524
<i>The more in tune with the times we were...</i>

8
00:01:47.358 --> 00:01:49.902
<i>...the more we drank.</i>

9
00:01:50.737 --> 00:01:54.657
<i>And none of us contributed anything new.</i>

10
00:01:57.035 --> 00:02:00.538
When I came back from New York,
I was disgusted.

11
00:02:03.249 --> 00:02:05.710
I see, Mr. Carraway.

12
00:02:05.918 --> 00:02:10.089
Disgusted with everyone and everything.

13
00:02:11.507 --> 00:02:15.094
Only one man was exempt from my disgust.

14
00:02:19.432 --> 00:02:21.142
One man?

15
00:02:22.018 --> 00:02:23.811
Mr. Carraway?

16
00:02:27.607 --> 00:02:29.067
Gatsby.

17
00:02:32.153 --> 00:02:34.030
Was he a friend of yours?

18
00:02:36.407 --> 00:02:37.867
He was...

19
00:02:39.118 --> 00:02:42.622
...the single most hopeful person
I've ever met.

20
00:02:44.457 --> 00:02:46.918
And am ever likely to meet again.

21
00:02:49.962 --> 00:02:50.842
There was something...

22
00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:53.841
...about him, a sensitivity.

23
00:02:54.050 --> 00:02:55.968
He was like...

24
00:02:56.177 --> 00:03:01.015
He was like one of those machines that
register earthquakes 10,000 miles away.

25
00:03:02.308 --> 00:03:03.685
Where'd you meet him?

26
00:03:09.107 --> 00:03:11.943
At a... At a party...

27
00:03:12.652 --> 00:03:14.362
...in New York.

28
00:03:14.570 --> 00:03:16.781
<i>In the summer of 1922...</i>

29
00:03:16.989 --> 00:03:19.909
<i>...the tempo of the city approached...</i>

30
00:03:20.076 --> 00:03:21.536
<i>...hysteria.</i>

31
00:03:33.756 --> 00:03:34.966
<i>Stocks reached...</i>

32
00:03:35.174 --> 00:03:37.051
<i>...record peaks...</i>

33
00:03:37.260 --> 00:03:38.094
<i>...and Wall Street boomed...</i>

34
00:03:38.261 --> 00:03:39.220
<i>...in a steady...</i>

35
00:03:39.387 --> 00:03:40.179
<i>golden roar.</i>

36
00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:43.766
<i>The parties were bigger.</i>

37
00:03:44.350 --> 00:03:45.309
<i>The shows...</i>

38
00:03:45.518 --> 00:03:46.978
<i>...were broader.</i>

39
00:03:47.186 --> 00:03:48.938
<i>The buildings were higher.</i>

40
00:03:49.647 --> 00:03:51.566
<i>The morals were looser and the ban...</i>

41
00:03:51.733 --> 00:03:53.818
<i>...on alcohol had backfired...</i>

42
00:03:54.068 --> 00:03:56.738
<i>...making the liquor cheaper.</i>

43
00:04:01.325 --> 00:04:05.455
<i>Wall Street was luring
the young and ambitious.</i>

44
00:04:06.664 --> 00:04:08.791
<i>And I was one of them.</i>

45
00:04:12.503 --> 00:04:15.673
<i>I rented a house 20 miles
from the city on Long Island.</i>

46
00:04:15.882 --> 00:04:17.383
<i>I lived at West Egg...</i>

47
00:04:17.592 --> 00:04:21.012
<i>...in a forgotten groundskeeper's cottage,
squeezed among the mansions...</i>

48
00:04:21.220 --> 00:04:22.930
<i>...of the newly rich.</i>

49
00:04:23.556 --> 00:04:27.810
<i>To get started, I bought a dozen volumes
on credit, banking and investments.</i>

50
00:04:28.019 --> 00:04:29.270
<i>All new to me.</i>

51
00:04:29.479 --> 00:04:30.239
<i>The stock market...</i>

52
00:04:30.313 --> 00:04:32.514
<i>- ...hit another high.</i>
- The market's moving up, up, up!

53
00:04:32.565 --> 00:04:36.819
Well, of course, nothing is 100 percent.
I wouldn't go investing every penny.

54
00:04:37.028 --> 00:04:39.530
<i>At Yale I dreamed of being a writer...</i>

55
00:04:39.697 --> 00:04:41.657
<i>...but I gave all that up.</i>

56
00:04:42.116 --> 00:04:43.993
<i>With the sun shining...</i>

57
00:04:44.202 --> 00:04:46.162
<i>...and the bursts of leaves on the trees...</i>

58
00:04:46.370 --> 00:04:47.330
<i>...I planned to spend...</i>

59
00:04:47.538 --> 00:04:48.915
<i>...the summer studying.</i>

60
00:04:50.416 --> 00:04:51.709
<i>And I probably would have...</i>

61
00:04:51.918 --> 00:04:53.544
<i>...were it not...</i>

62
00:04:53.753 --> 00:04:54.593
<i>...for the riotous...</i>

63
00:04:54.754 --> 00:04:56.339
<i>...amusements that beckoned...</i>

64
00:04:59.091 --> 00:04:59.884
<i>...from beyond...</i>

65
00:05:00.092 --> 00:05:01.844
<i>...the walls of that...</i>

66
00:05:02.178 --> 00:05:03.054
<i>...colossal castle...</i>

67
00:05:05.264 --> 00:05:07.391
<i>...owned by a gentleman I had...</i>

68
00:05:07.600 --> 00:05:09.268
<i>...not yet met...</i>

69
00:05:09.811 --> 00:05:12.313
<i>...named Gatsby.</i>

70
00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:14.941
<i>So...</i>

71
00:05:15.107 --> 00:05:16.984
...he was your neighbor.

72
00:05:17.443 --> 00:05:18.861
My neighbor.

73
00:05:19.821 --> 00:05:20.988
Yeah.

74
00:05:22.156 --> 00:05:26.494
When I think about it,
the history of the summer really began...

75
00:05:26.702 --> 00:05:29.831
<i>...the night I drove over
to my cousin Daisy's for dinner.</i>

76
00:05:30.039 --> 00:05:35.002
<i>She lived across the bay in old moneyed...</i>

77
00:05:35.878 --> 00:05:37.463
<i>...East Egg.</i>

78
00:05:41.926 --> 00:05:46.389
<i>Her husband was heir to
one of America's wealthiest families.</i>

79
00:05:46.806 --> 00:05:47.473
<i>His name...</i>

80
00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:49.934
<i>...was Tom...</i>

81
00:05:50.142 --> 00:05:51.143
<i>...Buchanan.</i>

82
00:05:53.312 --> 00:05:53.980
<i>When we were...</i>

83
00:05:54.146 --> 00:05:55.731
<i>...at Yale together, he'd been...</i>

84
00:05:55.898 --> 00:05:59.610
<i>...a sporting star. But now his glory days
were behind him and he...</i>

85
00:05:59.819 --> 00:06:01.904
<i>- ...contented himself with...</i>
- Telephone, Monsieur Buchanan.

86
00:06:02.113 --> 00:06:03.674
- It's me.
<i>- ...other affairs.</i>

87
00:06:03.739 --> 00:06:05.658
I thought I told you not to call me here.

88
00:06:05.867 --> 00:06:06.742
Boaz!

89
00:06:07.827 --> 00:06:08.995
Shakespeare!

90
00:06:09.203 --> 00:06:11.080
- Tom! Oh!
- Ha-ha-ha!

91
00:06:12.331 --> 00:06:16.252
- How's the great American novel coming?
- I'm selling bonds with Walter Chase's outfit.

92
00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:18.796
Let's say after dinner,
you and I, we go into town.

93
00:06:19.005 --> 00:06:20.339
- I can't.
- Catch up with the old wolf pack.

94
00:06:20.548 --> 00:06:23.509
- Big day on the job tomorrow.
- Nonsense! We're going.

95
00:06:23.926 --> 00:06:26.429
First team, all-American.

96
00:06:26.637 --> 00:06:27.305
You see?

97
00:06:27.513 --> 00:06:28.890
Made me who I am today.

98
00:06:29.098 --> 00:06:30.349
Forest Hills.

99
00:06:30.558 --> 00:06:32.159
Played the Prince of Wales.
What a sissy.

100
00:06:32.768 --> 00:06:33.436
Life is...

101
00:06:33.644 --> 00:06:34.896
...something you dominate.

102
00:06:35.062 --> 00:06:35.938
If you're any good.

103
00:06:36.689 --> 00:06:37.356
Oh!

104
00:06:43.404 --> 00:06:44.530
Oh.

105
00:06:50.328 --> 00:06:51.037
Hey.

106
00:07:01.631 --> 00:07:02.715
Henri!

107
00:07:02.924 --> 00:07:04.508
Where are you?

108
00:07:05.426 --> 00:07:06.093
The doors.

109
00:07:06.302 --> 00:07:07.553
Close them.

110
00:07:10.306 --> 00:07:11.933
Sorry. Thank you.

111
00:07:12.767 --> 00:07:14.977
Is that you, my lovely?

112
00:07:18.940 --> 00:07:21.859
<i>Daisy Buchanan, the golden girl.</i>

113
00:07:22.735 --> 00:07:23.569
<i>A breathless...</i>

114
00:07:23.778 --> 00:07:24.987
<i>...warmth flowed from her.</i>

115
00:07:25.154 --> 00:07:26.989
<i>A promise that there was no one else...</i>

116
00:07:27.198 --> 00:07:28.532
<i>...in the world she...</i>

117
00:07:28.741 --> 00:07:29.742
<i>...so wanted to see.</i>

118
00:07:29.951 --> 00:07:31.869
Do they miss me in Chicago?

119
00:07:32.119 --> 00:07:35.915
Yes. Um, at least a dozen people
send their love.

120
00:07:36.457 --> 00:07:37.792
How gorgeous.

121
00:07:38.292 --> 00:07:40.086
They're absolutely in mourning.

122
00:07:40.294 --> 00:07:41.574
- They're crying. Yes.
- No.

123
00:07:41.671 --> 00:07:42.951
- I don't believe you.
- Wailing.

124
00:07:43.047 --> 00:07:44.298
- I don't believe you.
- They're screaming.

125
00:07:44.507 --> 00:07:45.675
"Daisy Buchanan...

126
00:07:45.883 --> 00:07:46.643
...we can't live...

127
00:07:46.717 --> 00:07:49.887
- ...without you!"
- I'm paralyzed with happiness.

128
00:07:50.054 --> 00:07:50.721
Whoa!

129
00:07:51.263 --> 00:07:51.931
Whoa! Oh!

130
00:07:54.767 --> 00:07:55.434
Jordan Baker...

131
00:07:55.601 --> 00:07:56.268
...a very...

132
00:07:56.435 --> 00:07:57.520
...famous golfer.

133
00:07:57.728 --> 00:07:58.729
Oh.

134
00:07:58.938 --> 00:08:01.649
<i>She was the most frightening person
I'd ever seen.</i>

135
00:08:01.857 --> 00:08:04.443
Well, I've seen your face on the cover
of <i>Sporting Life.</i>

136
00:08:04.652 --> 00:08:06.112
Nick Carraway.

137
00:08:07.029 --> 00:08:08.614
<i>But I enjoyed looking at her.</i>

138
00:08:08.823 --> 00:08:10.424
I've been lying on that sofa...

139
00:08:10.491 --> 00:08:11.867
...for as long as I can remember.

140
00:08:12.159 --> 00:08:15.663
This summer I'll fling you two together.
I'll push you into linen closets...

141
00:08:15.871 --> 00:08:17.289
...and out to sea in boats!

142
00:08:17.498 --> 00:08:18.916
- I'm not listening to a word.
- So, Nick...

143
00:08:19.125 --> 00:08:20.001
...Daisy tells me...

144
00:08:20.167 --> 00:08:21.528
...that you're over in West Egg...

145
00:08:22.420 --> 00:08:23.460
...throwing your lot in...

146
00:08:23.546 --> 00:08:24.338
...with those social-climbing...

147
00:08:24.505 --> 00:08:25.798
...primitive new-money types.

148
00:08:26.007 --> 00:08:28.467
My little shack's just a cardboard box
at 80 a month.

149
00:08:28.676 --> 00:08:30.428
Your life is adorable.

150
00:08:30.636 --> 00:08:31.757
I know somebody in West Egg.

151
00:08:31.887 --> 00:08:32.805
I don't know a single person...

152
00:08:32.972 --> 00:08:34.432
...that side of the bay.

153
00:08:34.640 --> 00:08:36.225
You must know Gatsby.

154
00:08:37.435 --> 00:08:38.769
Gatsby?

155
00:08:42.940 --> 00:08:43.941
What Gatsby?

156
00:08:44.150 --> 00:08:46.235
Madame, the dinner is <i>servi.</i>

157
00:08:57.955 --> 00:08:58.956
<i>Would you like to hear...</i>

158
00:08:59.165 --> 00:09:00.624
<i>...a family secret?</i>

159
00:09:00.833 --> 00:09:02.752
- That's why I came over.
- It's about the butler's nose.

160
00:09:02.960 --> 00:09:04.336
Things went from bad to worse.

161
00:09:04.503 --> 00:09:06.172
I hate that word "hulking."

162
00:09:06.380 --> 00:09:08.049
I heard a rumor
that you were getting married...

163
00:09:08.215 --> 00:09:09.855
- ...to a girl out West.
- It's a libel.

164
00:09:10.009 --> 00:09:11.260
- I'm too poor.
- They have to be old...

165
00:09:11.427 --> 00:09:12.470
...so they die quickly.

166
00:09:12.678 --> 00:09:13.679
Can't we talk about something else?

167
00:09:13.888 --> 00:09:15.097
Anything. Crops.

168
00:09:15.306 --> 00:09:16.746
You're making me feel uncivilized...

169
00:09:16.766 --> 00:09:17.433
...Daisy.

170
00:09:17.641 --> 00:09:19.143
Civilization's going to pieces.

171
00:09:20.728 --> 00:09:23.064
<i>Have you read
The Rise of the Colored Empires...</i>

172
00:09:23.272 --> 00:09:25.858
...by this fellow Goddard?
Everybody ought to...

173
00:09:26.067 --> 00:09:28.235
...read it. The idea is...

174
00:09:28.444 --> 00:09:30.571
...that it's up to us, the dominant race...

175
00:09:30.780 --> 00:09:31.864
...to watch out or these...

176
00:09:32.448 --> 00:09:33.532
...other races...

177
00:09:33.699 --> 00:09:34.700
...will have control of things.

178
00:09:34.909 --> 00:09:38.829
Tom's very profound lately. He reads
deep books with long words in them.

179
00:09:39.163 --> 00:09:40.084
It's been proved.

180
00:09:40.247 --> 00:09:41.290
It's scientific.

181
00:09:41.499 --> 00:09:43.542
We've got to beat them down.

182
00:09:52.468 --> 00:09:53.594
Buchanan residence.

183
00:09:54.887 --> 00:09:56.639
Monsieur Wilson, from the garage.

184
00:09:57.807 --> 00:09:59.016
Monsieur Buchanan.

185
00:09:59.225 --> 00:10:01.268
Excuse me, I'll be right back.

186
00:10:05.022 --> 00:10:06.482
I'm sorry.

187
00:10:09.110 --> 00:10:11.987
- Well, this Mr. Gatsby you spoke of...
- I'm working on it.

188
00:10:12.196 --> 00:10:14.824
- ...he's my neighbor.
- Shh! Don't talk.

189
00:10:15.032 --> 00:10:16.325
I wanna hear what happens.

190
00:10:16.534 --> 00:10:17.934
I don't care what you do...

191
00:10:17.993 --> 00:10:18.953
Something happening?

192
00:10:19.161 --> 00:10:21.872
- Why, I thought everybody knew.
- Well, I don't.

193
00:10:22.081 --> 00:10:23.121
Tom's got some...

194
00:10:23.290 --> 00:10:25.084
- ...woman in New York.
- Got some woman?

195
00:10:25.292 --> 00:10:29.255
She might have the decency not to telephone
at dinnertime. Don't you think?

196
00:10:30.548 --> 00:10:33.300
- Is that too much to ask?
- Daisy, don't create a scene.

197
00:10:33.592 --> 00:10:34.969
I love seeing you at my table.

198
00:10:36.137 --> 00:10:38.430
You remind me of a rose.
An absolute rose...

199
00:10:38.639 --> 00:10:40.266
- ...doesn't he?
- So after dinner...

200
00:10:40.474 --> 00:10:41.433
I'm not like a rose.

201
00:10:41.642 --> 00:10:43.082
...Nick wanted to go into town.

202
00:10:43.102 --> 00:10:43.978
To the Yale Club.

203
00:10:44.186 --> 00:10:45.146
Nicky, stay.

204
00:10:45.354 --> 00:10:46.021
I have to work early.

205
00:10:46.188 --> 00:10:46.981
Nonsense.

206
00:10:47.189 --> 00:10:49.030
- There's so much to talk about.
- It's just...

207
00:10:49.150 --> 00:10:50.030
...for a drink or two.

208
00:10:56.574 --> 00:10:57.375
<i>None of us...</i>

209
00:10:57.491 --> 00:10:59.952
<i>...could ignore that
fifth guest's shrill...</i>

210
00:11:00.161 --> 00:11:02.246
<i>...metallic urgency.</i>

211
00:11:04.248 --> 00:11:06.041
- Nicky.
- What?

212
00:11:06.250 --> 00:11:10.546
It's just, well, you see,
I think everything's terrible anyhow.

213
00:11:11.046 --> 00:11:12.965
- Really?
- Yes.

214
00:11:13.174 --> 00:11:16.302
I've been everywhere and seen
everything and done everything.

215
00:11:16.635 --> 00:11:19.138
I've had a very bad time, Nicky.

216
00:11:19.346 --> 00:11:21.932
I'm pretty cynical about everything.

217
00:11:25.978 --> 00:11:27.438
Your daughter, I suppose she...

218
00:11:27.605 --> 00:11:29.273
...talks and eats and everything?

219
00:11:29.481 --> 00:11:30.608
Pammy?

220
00:11:31.692 --> 00:11:32.985
Oh, yes.

221
00:11:33.277 --> 00:11:36.572
Listen, Nick, when she was born...

222
00:11:36.989 --> 00:11:40.826
...Tom was God knows where...

223
00:11:45.623 --> 00:11:47.666
...with God knows whom.

224
00:11:48.167 --> 00:11:48.834
And I asked...

225
00:11:49.043 --> 00:11:51.670
...the nurse if it was a boy or a girl.

226
00:11:53.672 --> 00:11:55.341
And she said it was a girl...

227
00:11:56.008 --> 00:11:57.384
...and I wept:

228
00:11:57.968 --> 00:11:59.762
"I'm glad it's a girl.

229
00:12:02.181 --> 00:12:03.974
And I hope she'll be a fool.

230
00:12:04.975 --> 00:12:07.686
That's the best thing
a girl in this world can be.

231
00:12:09.230 --> 00:12:11.732
A beautiful little fool."

232
00:12:14.568 --> 00:12:18.739
All the bright, precious
things fade so fast.

233
00:12:20.699 --> 00:12:22.618
And they don't come back.

234
00:12:53.691 --> 00:12:55.234
<i>When I arrived home...</i>

235
00:12:55.442 --> 00:12:56.819
<i>...I noticed that a figure...</i>

236
00:12:57.027 --> 00:12:59.488
<i>...had emerged on my neighbor's dock.</i>

237
00:13:04.576 --> 00:13:06.578
<i>And something told me it</i> was...

238
00:13:07.705 --> 00:13:09.373
<i>...Mr. Gatsby.</i>

239
00:13:17.423 --> 00:13:19.466
<i>He seemed to be reaching toward...</i>

240
00:13:19.675 --> 00:13:21.802
<i>...something out there in the dark.</i>

241
00:13:35.065 --> 00:13:36.567
The green light.

242
00:13:42.031 --> 00:13:44.325
I don't wanna talk about this, doctor.

243
00:13:44.533 --> 00:13:45.826
Then write about it.

244
00:13:47.703 --> 00:13:49.997
- Write about it?
- Yes.

245
00:13:50.956 --> 00:13:52.291
Why would I do that?

246
00:13:53.125 --> 00:13:54.752
You said yourself writing...

247
00:13:54.960 --> 00:13:55.919
...brought you solace.

248
00:13:56.503 --> 00:14:01.050
Yeah, well, it didn't bring
anyone else much solace.

249
00:14:01.759 --> 00:14:03.469
I wasn't any good.

250
00:14:03.844 --> 00:14:05.346
No one need ever read it.

251
00:14:05.721 --> 00:14:07.389
You could always burn it.

252
00:14:12.686 --> 00:14:14.146
What would I write about?

253
00:14:14.521 --> 00:14:15.898
Anything.

254
00:14:17.107 --> 00:14:18.400
Whatever brings you ease:

255
00:14:19.568 --> 00:14:21.195
a memory...

256
00:14:21.403 --> 00:14:23.072
...a thought, a place.

257
00:14:26.200 --> 00:14:27.743
Write it down.

258
00:14:31.663 --> 00:14:33.165
A place.

259
00:14:39.171 --> 00:14:42.800
The Valley of Ashes was a grotesque place.

260
00:14:45.052 --> 00:14:49.681
<i>New York's dumping ground
halfway between West Egg and the city...</i>

261
00:14:49.890 --> 00:14:52.226
<i>...where the burnt-out coal that powered...</i>

262
00:14:52.434 --> 00:14:54.645
<i>...the booming golden city...</i>

263
00:14:54.853 --> 00:14:57.064
<i>...was discarded by men who...</i>

264
00:14:57.272 --> 00:14:59.024
<i>...moved dimly and already...</i>

265
00:14:59.233 --> 00:15:01.568
<i>...crumbling through the powdery air.</i>

266
00:15:02.569 --> 00:15:04.113
<i>This fantastic farm...</i>

267
00:15:04.321 --> 00:15:07.324
<i>...was ever watched by Dr. T.J.
Eckleburg...</i>

268
00:15:07.533 --> 00:15:11.703
<i>...a forgotten oculist
whose eyes brooded over it all...</i>

269
00:15:11.912 --> 00:15:13.163
<i>...like the eyes...</i>

270
00:15:13.580 --> 00:15:14.665
<i>...of God.</i>

271
00:15:17.334 --> 00:15:20.838
<i>Tom had invited me to town,
apparently for lunch at the Yale Club...</i>

272
00:15:21.004 --> 00:15:21.839
<i>...but...</i>

273
00:15:22.047 --> 00:15:22.923
<i>...the day took...</i>

274
00:15:23.132 --> 00:15:23.892
<i>...an unexpected...</i>

275
00:15:24.007 --> 00:15:25.175
<i>...turn.</i>

276
00:15:25.968 --> 00:15:26.969
Come on.

277
00:15:28.053 --> 00:15:29.388
- Come on!
- What do you mean?

278
00:15:29.596 --> 00:15:30.556
Trust me!

279
00:15:30.764 --> 00:15:32.965
- What are we doing?
- Where are you going?

280
00:15:33.058 --> 00:15:33.725
Jump!

281
00:15:33.934 --> 00:15:35.602
- What are you doing?
- Jump, come on!

282
00:15:35.811 --> 00:15:36.812
- Tom!
- Come on!

283
00:15:37.646 --> 00:15:39.148
Oh, God.

284
00:15:39.356 --> 00:15:42.067
Tom, wait. Wait a second, would you?

285
00:15:42.526 --> 00:15:43.366
Dominate, Nick!

286
00:15:43.652 --> 00:15:45.028
Dominate!

287
00:15:47.322 --> 00:15:48.824
Hello, Wilson.

288
00:15:49.032 --> 00:15:50.534
How's business?

289
00:15:51.785 --> 00:15:53.120
Yeah, I can't complain.

290
00:15:53.287 --> 00:15:54.121
Uh...

291
00:15:55.456 --> 00:15:57.082
So when are you gonna...

292
00:15:57.291 --> 00:16:00.586
- ...sell me that car?
- Oh, I've still got my man working on it.

293
00:16:00.794 --> 00:16:02.838
Yeah, well, he works pretty slow, don't he?

294
00:16:03.046 --> 00:16:04.882
Maybe I'd better sell it somewhere else.

295
00:16:05.090 --> 00:16:06.133
Oh, no, no, no.

296
00:16:06.341 --> 00:16:07.759
I wasn't saying that. I was...

297
00:16:07.968 --> 00:16:09.219
If it's business, you should be talking...

298
00:16:09.386 --> 00:16:10.387
...to me.

299
00:16:10.846 --> 00:16:11.680
Get some chairs...

300
00:16:11.847 --> 00:16:14.433
...why don't you,
so somebody can sit down.

301
00:16:16.018 --> 00:16:17.311
Uh, sure.

302
00:16:18.395 --> 00:16:19.771
Yeah, let's talk business.

303
00:16:20.481 --> 00:16:21.231
Sure.

304
00:16:21.440 --> 00:16:22.483
I'll get the chairs.

305
00:16:23.400 --> 00:16:24.193
Myrtle...

306
00:16:24.401 --> 00:16:26.161
- ...why don't you entertain?
- Hurry up.

307
00:16:27.362 --> 00:16:28.864
- Hi.
- Hi.

308
00:16:29.907 --> 00:16:30.787
Mr. Buchanan.

309
00:16:30.991 --> 00:16:31.700
Candy?

310
00:16:31.909 --> 00:16:33.702
- No, thank you.
- No?

311
00:16:33.911 --> 00:16:35.329
Mrs. Wilson, Nick Carraway.

312
00:16:35.496 --> 00:16:36.330
Oh.

313
00:16:36.497 --> 00:16:37.498
A pleasure.

314
00:16:37.706 --> 00:16:38.749
Nick's a writer.

315
00:16:38.916 --> 00:16:39.750
Oh.

316
00:16:39.958 --> 00:16:41.084
I'm in bonds actually.

317
00:16:41.460 --> 00:16:42.836
I want you...

318
00:16:44.296 --> 00:16:45.547
...get on the next train.

319
00:16:45.756 --> 00:16:46.423
Now?

320
00:16:46.632 --> 00:16:47.799
Yes.

321
00:16:48.008 --> 00:16:49.009
Can we get the dog?

322
00:16:49.218 --> 00:16:51.011
- For the apartment?
- Whatever you want.

323
00:16:51.220 --> 00:16:52.095
Hey, Mr. Buchanan!

324
00:16:52.304 --> 00:16:53.388
You want a soda?

325
00:16:53.555 --> 00:16:54.473
- I'm fine.
- No?

326
00:16:56.391 --> 00:16:58.143
Call your sister. She'll like him.

327
00:16:58.352 --> 00:17:00.187
No, no, no. That's all right, thank you.

328
00:17:00.395 --> 00:17:02.755
Catherine's said to be very good-looking
by people who know.

329
00:17:02.856 --> 00:17:03.982
Oh, really, I can't.

330
00:17:04.191 --> 00:17:04.983
Hey.

331
00:17:05.192 --> 00:17:06.902
You wanna embarrass Myrtle?

332
00:17:08.946 --> 00:17:10.072
That's rude.

333
00:17:49.570 --> 00:17:50.445
I'm Catherine.

334
00:17:50.654 --> 00:17:52.614
Ain't we having a party?

335
00:17:53.156 --> 00:17:54.908
Um, I'm not sure...

336
00:17:55.117 --> 00:17:55.957
...now's a good time.

337
00:17:56.076 --> 00:17:58.078
I'm just going. Actually, there are peop...

338
00:17:58.287 --> 00:17:59.246
Hello!

339
00:17:59.454 --> 00:18:01.164
Oh, Chester, this must be the cousin.

340
00:18:01.373 --> 00:18:03.542
- Oh, you are adorable.
- Oh, thank you.

341
00:18:03.750 --> 00:18:05.294
Chester McKee. Pleasure to meet you.

342
00:18:05.502 --> 00:18:07.142
- Nick Carraway.
- Come on...

343
00:18:07.254 --> 00:18:09.089
- ...don't you like me?
- Oh. Heh. A plant.

344
00:18:09.548 --> 00:18:10.299
Myrtle!

345
00:18:11.049 --> 00:18:12.426
Myrtle!

346
00:18:16.513 --> 00:18:17.723
Myrtle turtle!

347
00:18:19.891 --> 00:18:21.226
I really must go.

348
00:18:21.435 --> 00:18:23.236
Get everybody a drink
before they fall asleep.

349
00:18:23.312 --> 00:18:24.813
Torn, I'm just leaving now.

350
00:18:25.480 --> 00:18:26.231
Nick.

351
00:18:26.398 --> 00:18:27.065
Wait.

352
00:18:27.274 --> 00:18:29.568
- I'm going. I've gotta get out of here.
- Nonsense!

353
00:18:29.776 --> 00:18:32.112
Go on in there and talk to Catherine.

354
00:18:32.321 --> 00:18:33.947
I'm not comfortable. Daisy's my cousin.

355
00:18:34.156 --> 00:18:37.284
Listen, I know you like to watch.
I remember that from college.

356
00:18:37.492 --> 00:18:39.369
No, no, no, I don't make any judgment.

357
00:18:39.578 --> 00:18:40.746
We have all summer.

358
00:18:40.954 --> 00:18:45.292
Now, do you wanna sit on the sideline
and watch, or do you wanna play ball?

359
00:18:45.500 --> 00:18:46.501
Play ball.

360
00:18:46.710 --> 00:18:48.795
- Ain't we good enough for you?
- Come on!

361
00:18:49.254 --> 00:18:51.381
Come on! Come on.

362
00:18:51.590 --> 00:18:54.134
He's gonna sit on the side and watch, huh?

363
00:18:54.343 --> 00:18:55.594
Or is he gonna play ball?

364
00:18:56.345 --> 00:18:58.847
Take off your hat and stay a while.

365
00:18:59.056 --> 00:19:00.682
Oh, hey, Nick. McKee...

366
00:19:00.891 --> 00:19:02.684
- ...is in the artistic game.
- Photography.

367
00:19:02.893 --> 00:19:04.394
- Nick's artistic.
- No.

368
00:19:04.603 --> 00:19:06.063
- No, no, no.
- Really?

369
00:19:06.772 --> 00:19:09.024
- I write a little, but...
- Really?

370
00:19:09.232 --> 00:19:10.776
Do you live on Long Island too?

371
00:19:10.984 --> 00:19:13.070
- I live at West Egg.
- I was there at a party...

372
00:19:13.278 --> 00:19:16.990
...about a month ago. A man named Gatsbys.
Do you know him?

373
00:19:18.075 --> 00:19:19.660
I live right next door to him.

374
00:19:20.410 --> 00:19:22.579
He's a cousin of Kaiser Wilhelm's.

375
00:19:22.913 --> 00:19:24.790
- You know, the evil German king?
- Really?

376
00:19:25.415 --> 00:19:26.166
Hey, McKee!

377
00:19:27.250 --> 00:19:28.690
- Take a picture of that.
- Ah!

378
00:19:30.003 --> 00:19:31.630
Don't, I'm not one of those models.

379
00:19:32.714 --> 00:19:33.715
You can if you want.

380
00:19:37.344 --> 00:19:40.097
Neither of them can stand
the person they're married to.

381
00:19:40.972 --> 00:19:42.432
Doesn't she like Wilson either?

382
00:19:42.641 --> 00:19:43.641
He's a greasy...

383
00:19:43.725 --> 00:19:44.643
...little scumbag.

384
00:19:44.851 --> 00:19:47.771
No, thanks, I feel just as good
on nothing at all.

385
00:19:48.438 --> 00:19:49.481
Nerve pills.

386
00:19:49.815 --> 00:19:51.566
I get them from a doctor in Queens.

387
00:19:52.067 --> 00:19:52.943
Do you want one?

388
00:19:53.360 --> 00:19:55.237
Oh, no. My nerves are fine, thanks.

389
00:20:05.747 --> 00:20:08.959
<i>I had been drunk just twice in my life.</i>

390
00:20:09.334 --> 00:20:13.839
<i>And the second time was that afternoon.</i>

391
00:20:56.256 --> 00:20:57.466
<i>That night, in the hidden...</i>

392
00:20:57.674 --> 00:21:00.218
<i>...flat that Tom kept for Myrtle...</i>

393
00:21:00.427 --> 00:21:02.053
<i>...we were buoyed by a sort of...</i>

394
00:21:02.262 --> 00:21:03.138
<i>...chemical madness.</i>

395
00:21:04.389 --> 00:21:05.307
<i>A willingness...</i>

396
00:21:05.515 --> 00:21:06.892
<i>...of the heart that burst...</i>

397
00:21:07.100 --> 00:21:08.393
<i>...thunderously upon us all.</i>

398
00:21:09.686 --> 00:21:10.854
<i>And suddenly, I began...</i>

399
00:21:11.062 --> 00:21:12.564
<i>...to like New York.</i>

400
00:21:12.773 --> 00:21:14.649
This is better than the Yale Club.

401
00:21:47.015 --> 00:21:47.682
<i>High...</i>

402
00:21:47.849 --> 00:21:49.267
<i>...over the city...</i>

403
00:21:49.726 --> 00:21:50.727
<i>...our yellow...</i>

404
00:21:50.894 --> 00:21:53.063
<i>...windows must have
contributed their share...</i>

405
00:21:53.230 --> 00:21:55.190
<i>...of human secrets...</i>

406
00:21:56.399 --> 00:21:57.234
<i>...to the casual...</i>

407
00:21:57.400 --> 00:21:59.402
<i>...watcher in the street.</i>

408
00:22:02.322 --> 00:22:03.073
<i>And I was him...</i>

409
00:22:03.281 --> 00:22:04.574
<i>...too...</i>

410
00:22:04.783 --> 00:22:06.326
<i>...looking up and wondering.</i>

411
00:22:06.535 --> 00:22:08.119
<i>I was within...</i>

412
00:22:09.162 --> 00:22:10.789
<i>...and without.</i>

413
00:22:13.500 --> 00:22:16.086
<i>Enchanted and repelled...</i>

414
00:22:17.128 --> 00:22:21.508
<i>...b y the inexhaustible variety of life.</i>

415
00:22:29.516 --> 00:22:30.716
You have got no right...

416
00:22:30.892 --> 00:22:32.185
...to speak her name.

417
00:22:32.644 --> 00:22:33.603
Daisy, Daisy, Daisy!

418
00:22:33.770 --> 00:22:37.148
- You got no right to speak her name!
<i>- I'll</i> speak her name whenever...

419
00:22:40.527 --> 00:22:42.863
- Oh, my God, you are crazy!
- You whore!

420
00:22:43.029 --> 00:22:44.447
They're gonna arrest you!

421
00:22:58.587 --> 00:23:00.839
<i>I have no clue how I got home...</i>

422
00:23:01.047 --> 00:23:02.382
<i>...but I do know that I...</i>

423
00:23:02.632 --> 00:23:04.718
<i>...awoke with a distinctly...</i>

424
00:23:04.926 --> 00:23:05.927
<i>...uneasy feeling...</i>

425
00:23:06.136 --> 00:23:08.805
<i>...that Gatsby was watching me.</i>

426
00:23:11.057 --> 00:23:12.517
Watching you?

427
00:23:13.894 --> 00:23:14.895
Yes.

428
00:23:15.520 --> 00:23:16.187
<i>Gatsby...</i>

429
00:23:16.396 --> 00:23:18.440
<i>...was always watching me.</i>

430
00:23:19.649 --> 00:23:21.443
And how did you know that?

431
00:23:22.110 --> 00:23:23.820
I got an invitation.

432
00:23:26.114 --> 00:23:26.865
<i>I was...</i>

433
00:23:27.032 --> 00:23:28.199
...the only one.

434
00:23:28.408 --> 00:23:31.202
<i>By which I mean no one except me...</i>

435
00:23:31.411 --> 00:23:35.999
<i>...ever received an actual invitation
to Gatsby's.</i>

436
00:23:37.375 --> 00:23:38.655
<i>You see, the rest of New York...</i>

437
00:23:38.752 --> 00:23:41.546
<i>...simply came uninvited.</i>

438
00:23:44.049 --> 00:23:46.801
<i>The whole city packed into automobiles.</i>

439
00:23:46.968 --> 00:23:49.888
<i>And all weekend, every weekend...</i>

440
00:23:50.305 --> 00:23:53.391
<i>...ended up at Gatsby's.</i>

441
00:23:57.270 --> 00:23:58.730
Yeah!

442
00:24:02.025 --> 00:24:03.185
<i>And I mean everyone...</i>

443
00:24:04.277 --> 00:24:05.153
<i>...from every walk of life...</i>

444
00:24:05.362 --> 00:24:06.283
<i>...from every corner...</i>

445
00:24:06.404 --> 00:24:07.489
<i>...of New York City...</i>

446
00:24:07.697 --> 00:24:08.865
<i>...this kaleidoscopic.</i> ..

447
00:24:09.074 --> 00:24:10.075
<i>...carnival...</i>

448
00:24:10.492 --> 00:24:11.284
<i>...spilled...</i>

449
00:24:11.451 --> 00:24:14.037
<i>- ...through Gatsby's door.</i>
- Out of the way!

450
00:24:14.245 --> 00:24:15.413
My invitation. Oh.

451
00:24:22.420 --> 00:24:24.130
Sir, my invitation.

452
00:24:24.464 --> 00:24:25.423
This Way!

453
00:24:40.939 --> 00:24:42.482
Hey!

454
00:24:51.491 --> 00:24:52.412
<i>A caravanserai...</i>

455
00:24:52.492 --> 00:24:56.997
...of <i>billionaire playboy publishers
and their blond nurses.</i>

456
00:24:57.664 --> 00:25:01.167
<i>Heiresses comparing inheritances
on Gatsby's beach.</i>

457
00:25:01.376 --> 00:25:05.338
<i>My boss, Walter Chase,
losing money at the roulette tables.</i>

458
00:25:05.547 --> 00:25:07.799
<i>Gossip columnists alongside...</i>

459
00:25:08.008 --> 00:25:11.261
<i>gangsters and governors
exchanging telephone numbers.</i>

460
00:25:11.469 --> 00:25:13.054
<i>Film stars...</i>

461
00:25:13.263 --> 00:25:14.931
<i>...Broadway directors...</i>

462
00:25:15.140 --> 00:25:16.516
<i>...morality protectors...</i>

463
00:25:16.725 --> 00:25:18.351
<i>...high school defectors.</i>

464
00:25:18.560 --> 00:25:22.439
<i>And Ewing Klipspringer,
dubious descendant of Beethoven.</i>

465
00:25:27.944 --> 00:25:31.322
Do you know where I might find
the host, Mr. Gatsby? I live next door.

466
00:25:31.906 --> 00:25:32.666
Gatsby?

467
00:25:32.824 --> 00:25:33.905
I've never seen Mr. Gatsby.

468
00:25:34.451 --> 00:25:36.036
Why, no one has.

469
00:25:36.494 --> 00:25:37.162
<i>Alone...</i>

470
00:25:37.370 --> 00:25:38.371
<i>...and embarrassed...</i>

471
00:25:39.289 --> 00:25:40.081
<i>...I decided...</i>

472
00:25:40.290 --> 00:25:41.091
<i>...to get roaring...</i>

473
00:25:41.708 --> 00:25:42.500
<i>...drunk.</i>

474
00:26:10.862 --> 00:26:11.905
Wow.

475
00:26:12.072 --> 00:26:13.673
I thought I might see you here.

476
00:26:16.743 --> 00:26:18.828
- Hello.
- I remembered...

477
00:26:19.037 --> 00:26:19.917
...you live next door.

478
00:26:20.121 --> 00:26:21.414
It's like an amusement park.

479
00:26:21.623 --> 00:26:22.999
Shall we?

480
00:26:23.583 --> 00:26:25.424
- Did you get an invitation?
- People aren't...

481
00:26:25.502 --> 00:26:26.836
...invited to Gatsby's.

482
00:26:27.045 --> 00:26:28.129
Well, I was.

483
00:26:28.338 --> 00:26:29.839
Seems I'm the only one.

484
00:26:30.048 --> 00:26:31.341
Who is this Gatsby?

485
00:26:31.549 --> 00:26:34.552
- He was a German spy during the war.
- Teddy Barton...

486
00:26:34.761 --> 00:26:35.887
...Nick Carraway.

487
00:26:36.096 --> 00:26:37.347
A German spy?

488
00:26:37.555 --> 00:26:39.599
No, no, no. He's the Kaiser's assassin.

489
00:26:39.808 --> 00:26:41.476
I heard he killed a man once.

490
00:26:41.684 --> 00:26:43.644
- True.
- Kills for fun, free of charge.

491
00:26:43.686 --> 00:26:45.146
He's certainly richer than God.

492
00:26:46.815 --> 00:26:49.109
You don't really believe
he killed a man, do you?

493
00:26:49.317 --> 00:26:51.611
Let's go find him
and you can ask him yourself.

494
00:26:51.820 --> 00:26:54.405
Ladies and gentlemen,
please welcome to the stage...

495
00:26:54.614 --> 00:26:55.824
...the incredible...

496
00:26:56.032 --> 00:26:57.951
...Miss Gilda Gray!

497
00:27:03.915 --> 00:27:05.375
The Charleston!

498
00:27:05.583 --> 00:27:07.043
<i>At least I miss</i>

499
00:27:07.335 --> 00:27:08.628
<i>Trips around the world</i>

500
00:27:09.129 --> 00:27:10.255
<i>Don't mean a thing</i>

501
00:27:10.463 --> 00:27:12.257
<i>If I ain't your girl</i>

502
00:27:12.924 --> 00:27:13.883
<i>I ain't got time</i>

503
00:27:14.092 --> 00:27:15.552
<i>For you baby</i>

504
00:27:16.511 --> 00:27:18.513
<i>Either you're mine
Or you're not</i>

505
00:27:19.889 --> 00:27:21.474
Mr. Gatsby?

506
00:27:21.641 --> 00:27:22.475
<i>Sweet baby</i>

507
00:27:22.642 --> 00:27:23.601
Come on.

508
00:27:23.935 --> 00:27:25.145
<i>Right here, right now</i>

509
00:27:27.188 --> 00:27:28.565
But you are mistaken!

510
00:27:29.023 --> 00:27:30.150
For I am...

511
00:27:30.358 --> 00:27:31.276
...the mysterious...

512
00:27:31.484 --> 00:27:33.444
...Mr. Gatsby.

513
00:27:33.653 --> 00:27:35.113
You won't find him.

514
00:27:35.864 --> 00:27:36.948
This house...

515
00:27:38.283 --> 00:27:39.492
...and everything in it...

516
00:27:39.993 --> 00:27:40.827
...are all part...

517
00:27:41.035 --> 00:27:42.620
...of an elaborate disguise.

518
00:27:46.207 --> 00:27:48.918
But Mr. Gatsby doesn't exist.

519
00:27:50.962 --> 00:27:52.964
Phooey. I've met him.

520
00:27:53.173 --> 00:27:54.883
Really? Which one?

521
00:27:55.341 --> 00:27:56.467
The prince?

522
00:27:57.343 --> 00:27:58.761
The spy?

523
00:27:58.970 --> 00:28:00.471
The murderer?

524
00:28:00.930 --> 00:28:02.390
I cannot find anyone...

525
00:28:03.266 --> 00:28:05.476
...who knows anything real about...

526
00:28:05.685 --> 00:28:06.728
...Mr. Gatsby.

527
00:28:06.936 --> 00:28:08.730
Well, I don't care.

528
00:28:09.105 --> 00:28:10.440
He gives large parties...

529
00:28:10.648 --> 00:28:11.983
...and I like large parties.

530
00:28:12.192 --> 00:28:13.860
They're so intimate.

531
00:28:14.068 --> 00:28:16.613
Small parties, there isn't any privacy.

532
00:28:16.821 --> 00:28:18.406
But if that's true...

533
00:28:18.615 --> 00:28:20.283
...what's all this for?

534
00:28:20.700 --> 00:28:22.744
That, my dear fellow...

535
00:28:22.952 --> 00:28:24.913
...is the question.

536
00:28:25.455 --> 00:28:27.040
<i>A little party never</i>

537
00:28:27.248 --> 00:28:28.708
<i>Killed nobody</i>

538
00:28:28.875 --> 00:28:30.168
<i>So we gonna dance</i>

539
00:28:30.335 --> 00:28:32.086
<i>Until we drop</i>

540
00:28:32.962 --> 00:28:34.797
<i>A little party never killed</i>

541
00:28:35.006 --> 00:28:36.216
<i>Nobody</i>

542
00:28:36.382 --> 00:28:38.092
<i>Right here, right now
ls all we got</i>

543
00:28:40.386 --> 00:28:41.262
<i>A little party</i>

544
00:28:41.429 --> 00:28:42.847
<i>Never killed nobody</i>

545
00:28:44.098 --> 00:28:45.350
May I have this dance?

546
00:28:45.558 --> 00:28:46.879
You penniless pantywaist.

547
00:28:47.727 --> 00:28:49.604
<i>A little party never killed</i>

548
00:28:49.854 --> 00:28:50.521
Oh, yeah.

549
00:28:50.688 --> 00:28:51.356
<i>Nobody</i>

550
00:29:02.700 --> 00:29:03.540
I'm stealing...

551
00:29:03.576 --> 00:29:04.452
...her away.

552
00:29:05.536 --> 00:29:07.536
- Ladies and gentlemen...
- Come on.

553
00:29:07.705 --> 00:29:10.124
...a jazz history of the world...

554
00:29:10.625 --> 00:29:11.668
...and accompanying...

555
00:29:13.002 --> 00:29:14.545
...fireworks!

556
00:29:18.341 --> 00:29:20.426
- Come on. Nick.
- Look around you.

557
00:29:20.635 --> 00:29:22.762
Rich girls don't marry poor boys.

558
00:29:23.972 --> 00:29:25.056
She's mine.

559
00:29:25.265 --> 00:29:26.432
Your face is familiar.

560
00:29:26.641 --> 00:29:28.561
Weren't you in the Third Division
during the war?

561
00:29:28.726 --> 00:29:30.927
- Oh, yes, the 9th Battalion.
- I was in the 7th.

562
00:29:31.104 --> 00:29:33.940
I knew you looked familiar.
Having a good time, old sport?

563
00:29:34.148 --> 00:29:35.308
The whole thing's incredible.

564
00:29:35.483 --> 00:29:36.734
I live just next door.

565
00:29:38.194 --> 00:29:40.947
He sent me an actual invitation.
Seems I'm the only one.

566
00:29:41.155 --> 00:29:42.991
I still haven't met Mr. Gatsby.

567
00:29:43.199 --> 00:29:44.158
No one's met him.

568
00:29:44.367 --> 00:29:47.704
They say he's third cousin to the Kaiser
and second cousin to the devil.

569
00:29:47.912 --> 00:29:49.247
I'm afraid I haven't been
a very good host...

570
00:29:49.455 --> 00:29:50.206
...old sport.

571
00:29:50.873 --> 00:29:51.916
You see...

572
00:29:55.461 --> 00:29:56.879
...I'm Gatsby.

573
00:29:58.047 --> 00:29:58.715
You're...

574
00:30:03.553 --> 00:30:05.138
<i>His smile was one of those...</i>

575
00:30:05.346 --> 00:30:07.026
<i>...rare smiles that you may come across...</i>

576
00:30:07.140 --> 00:30:09.267
<i>...four or five times in life.</i>

577
00:30:09.684 --> 00:30:10.852
<i>It seemed to understand...</i>

578
00:30:11.060 --> 00:30:14.605
<i>you and believe in you
just as you would like to be understood...</i>

579
00:30:14.814 --> 00:30:16.232
<i>...and believed in.</i>

580
00:30:16.441 --> 00:30:18.401
Sorry, old sport. I thought you knew.

581
00:30:18.735 --> 00:30:21.195
Please just... I don't know what to say.

582
00:30:21.404 --> 00:30:23.614
- Please forgive me. I've had...
- It's quite all right.

583
00:30:23.823 --> 00:30:25.033
- ...so much to drink.
- Yes?

584
00:30:25.241 --> 00:30:26.326
Mr. Gatsby, sir.

585
00:30:26.534 --> 00:30:27.910
Chicago on the wire.

586
00:30:28.119 --> 00:30:29.412
I'll be in in just a minute.

587
00:30:29.620 --> 00:30:32.540
I'm taking my new hydroplane out
in the morning.

588
00:30:32.749 --> 00:30:34.334
Would you like to go with me?

589
00:30:35.168 --> 00:30:36.252
What time?

590
00:30:36.461 --> 00:30:37.962
The time that suits you.

591
00:30:38.171 --> 00:30:40.048
Well, that's very kind of you.

592
00:30:40.840 --> 00:30:43.259
Lovely to see you again, Miss Baker.

593
00:30:44.802 --> 00:30:45.511
If there's...

594
00:30:45.678 --> 00:30:47.513
...anything that you want...

595
00:30:47.722 --> 00:30:49.724
...just ask for it, old sport.

596
00:30:52.769 --> 00:30:54.437
Excuse me. I will...

597
00:30:54.645 --> 00:30:56.230
...rejoin you later.

598
00:31:01.903 --> 00:31:03.696
I expected him to be...

599
00:31:04.238 --> 00:31:06.115
- Old and fat?
- Yes.

600
00:31:06.949 --> 00:31:09.535
Young men don't just drift coolly
out of nowhere...

601
00:31:09.744 --> 00:31:11.871
...and buy a palace on Long Island.

602
00:31:13.623 --> 00:31:14.663
He told me once...

603
00:31:14.665 --> 00:31:16.793
...he was an Oxford man.

604
00:31:16.959 --> 00:31:18.086
However, I don't believe it.

605
00:31:18.920 --> 00:31:19.837
Why not?

606
00:31:20.046 --> 00:31:23.007
I don't know.
I just don't believe he went there.

607
00:31:23.174 --> 00:31:24.255
I beg your pardon.

608
00:31:25.259 --> 00:31:27.678
Miss Baker,
Mr. Gatsby would like to speak to you.

609
00:31:28.346 --> 00:31:29.680
Alone.

610
00:31:30.139 --> 00:31:30.807
Me?

611
00:31:31.432 --> 00:31:32.433
Yes, madam.

612
00:33:12.867 --> 00:33:14.035
Nick!

613
00:33:14.243 --> 00:33:15.411
Nick!

614
00:33:15.620 --> 00:33:16.829
Nick!

615
00:33:17.121 --> 00:33:19.332
I've just heard the most shocking thing.

616
00:33:19.790 --> 00:33:21.792
Where have you been? The car's waiting.

617
00:33:22.001 --> 00:33:24.629
Simply amazing. It all makes sense.

618
00:33:25.379 --> 00:33:27.381
It all makes sense.

619
00:33:27.715 --> 00:33:29.050
Come on.

620
00:33:29.425 --> 00:33:31.344
- What makes sense?
- Everything!

621
00:33:31.552 --> 00:33:33.221
Come on, this is crazy! We gotta...

622
00:33:33.429 --> 00:33:35.723
- ...get out of here.
- Here I am tantalizing you...

623
00:33:35.932 --> 00:33:36.933
...when I swore I wouldn't tell.

624
00:33:37.099 --> 00:33:38.226
Just tell me.

625
00:33:38.434 --> 00:33:40.144
Oh, Nick, I'm sorry, I swore.

626
00:33:40.353 --> 00:33:42.438
I swore I wouldn't tell.

627
00:33:48.736 --> 00:33:50.404
Sorry to keep her from you, old sport.

628
00:33:51.239 --> 00:33:52.949
- Ah.
- Don't forget...

629
00:33:53.157 --> 00:33:54.958
...we're going up
in that hydroplane tomorrow.

630
00:33:56.160 --> 00:33:57.119
Yes.

631
00:33:57.328 --> 00:33:58.621
Mr. Gatsby, sir...

632
00:33:58.829 --> 00:34:00.309
- ...Philadelphia on the phone.
- Yes.

633
00:34:02.917 --> 00:34:04.001
Night, old sport.

634
00:34:04.669 --> 00:34:05.836
Good night.

635
00:34:06.462 --> 00:34:07.129
Thank you.

636
00:34:13.928 --> 00:34:14.762
What's the matter?

637
00:34:14.971 --> 00:34:16.389
You run out of gas?

638
00:34:16.597 --> 00:34:18.724
Nick! Come and see me!

639
00:34:18.933 --> 00:34:21.102
We'll have tea next week.

640
00:34:21.310 --> 00:34:22.812
I'm in the phone book.

641
00:34:23.396 --> 00:34:24.897
I'll call you up.

642
00:34:55.136 --> 00:34:57.471
<i>Well, we rode in the hydroplane.</i>

643
00:34:57.680 --> 00:34:58.639
<i>And I attended...</i>

644
00:34:58.848 --> 00:35:00.641
<i>...two more of his parties.</i>

645
00:35:00.850 --> 00:35:03.060
<i>Even made use of his beach.</i>

646
00:35:03.811 --> 00:35:06.564
<i>But you know, doctor, I realized...</i>

647
00:35:06.731 --> 00:35:11.694
<i>...that I knew absolutely nothing
about Gatsby at all.</i>

648
00:35:12.903 --> 00:35:14.196
<i>Until...</i>

649
00:35:36.093 --> 00:35:38.929
It's pretty, isn't it, old sport?
Haven't you ever seen it before?

650
00:35:40.348 --> 00:35:41.974
It's all a custom job.

651
00:35:42.183 --> 00:35:43.225
Supercharged engine.

652
00:35:44.435 --> 00:35:45.561
Get dressed.

653
00:35:45.978 --> 00:35:47.188
We're going to lunch.

654
00:36:00.993 --> 00:36:02.912
- Oh, well.
- Yeah.

655
00:36:04.163 --> 00:36:05.363
Look here, old sport.

656
00:36:06.040 --> 00:36:08.334
What is your opinion of me, anyhow?

657
00:36:08.834 --> 00:36:09.710
- My opinion?
- Yes!

658
00:36:09.919 --> 00:36:10.961
Yes, your opinion.

659
00:36:12.797 --> 00:36:14.924
I don't want you
to get the wrong impression...

660
00:36:15.132 --> 00:36:17.802
...from all these bizarre accusations
you must be hearing.

661
00:36:18.010 --> 00:36:20.513
A pack of lies, I guarantee.
You've heard the stories?

662
00:36:20.680 --> 00:36:21.639
Oh.

663
00:36:21.889 --> 00:36:24.225
I will tell you God's truth.
God's truth about myself.

664
00:36:24.433 --> 00:36:27.061
I am the son of very wealthy people
from the Middle West.

665
00:36:27.269 --> 00:36:28.390
Sadly, all of them are dead.

666
00:36:30.940 --> 00:36:32.191
I was brought up in America...

667
00:36:32.400 --> 00:36:33.317
...but educated at Oxford...

668
00:36:33.484 --> 00:36:34.318
...because all my ancestors...

669
00:36:34.527 --> 00:36:37.196
...have been educated there for years.
It's a family tradition.

670
00:36:37.405 --> 00:36:39.281
<i>The way he spoke.</i>

671
00:36:39.490 --> 00:36:41.867
<i>No wonder people thought he was lying.</i>

672
00:36:42.076 --> 00:36:44.870
After my family died,
I ran into a great deal of money.

673
00:36:45.079 --> 00:36:46.831
After that, I lived like a prince...

674
00:36:47.039 --> 00:36:48.360
...in all the capitals of Europe.

675
00:36:48.916 --> 00:36:50.000
Oh, Europe.

676
00:36:50.209 --> 00:36:51.502
Yes, Europe.

677
00:36:51.711 --> 00:36:52.962
Paris, Venice...

678
00:36:53.170 --> 00:36:53.890
...Rome, Vienna...

679
00:36:54.046 --> 00:36:55.172
...Zurich, Helsinki...

680
00:36:55.381 --> 00:36:56.757
...Moscow, Istanbul...

681
00:36:56.966 --> 00:36:58.050
...collecting jewels...

682
00:36:58.259 --> 00:36:59.218
...chiefly rubies...

683
00:36:59.427 --> 00:37:00.136
...hunting big game...

684
00:37:00.302 --> 00:37:04.181
...painting a little, things for myself only.
Trying to forget something sad...

685
00:37:04.390 --> 00:37:05.550
<i>Just when I thought...</i>

686
00:37:05.641 --> 00:37:07.977
<i>...it couldn't be any more fantastical...</i>

687
00:37:08.185 --> 00:37:10.187
- Then came the war, old sport.
<i>- ...he became...</i>

688
00:37:10.396 --> 00:37:13.899
<i>...a war hero, single-handedly
defeating the German army.</i>

689
00:37:14.108 --> 00:37:17.236
In the Argonne Forest, I took two
machine gun detachments so far...

690
00:37:17.445 --> 00:37:18.988
We were outnumbered 5 to 1.

691
00:37:19.196 --> 00:37:20.489
There was a half a mile gap...

692
00:37:20.698 --> 00:37:22.783
There wasn't a single German soldier
left standing.

693
00:37:22.992 --> 00:37:24.285
We stayed there two days and two nights.

694
00:37:24.493 --> 00:37:25.493
...Saw were piles of dead.

695
00:37:25.494 --> 00:37:28.497
One hundred and thirty men
with only 16 Lewis guns.

696
00:37:28.706 --> 00:37:30.708
Every Allied government gave me a medal.

697
00:37:30.916 --> 00:37:31.667
Even Montenegro.

698
00:37:34.253 --> 00:37:35.004
Here.

699
00:37:35.171 --> 00:37:36.714
That's from Montenegro.

700
00:37:37.006 --> 00:37:38.799
"Major Jay Gatsby for...

701
00:37:39.008 --> 00:37:41.488
- ...valor extraordinary."
- Valor extraordinary. That's right.

702
00:37:41.552 --> 00:37:44.764
And this is something I always carry
with me, a souvenir of Oxford days.

703
00:37:45.306 --> 00:37:47.933
That was taken in Trinity quad.
The man on my left...

704
00:37:48.142 --> 00:37:50.936
- ...is now the Earl of Doncaster.
<i>- What could I say?</i>

705
00:37:52.104 --> 00:37:54.690
<i>The photograph was undoubtedly authentic.</i>

706
00:37:54.899 --> 00:37:57.109
<i>Could it all be true?</i>

707
00:37:57.318 --> 00:38:00.154
Of course, you don't need to take
my word for it, old sport.

708
00:38:00.362 --> 00:38:04.700
At lunch, I'm going to introduce you to one of
New York's most distinguished businessmen...

709
00:38:04.909 --> 00:38:07.161
...a Mr. Meyer Wolfshiem, my good friend.

710
00:38:07.369 --> 00:38:08.889
He will confirm all I have told you...

711
00:38:09.079 --> 00:38:11.479
- ...and vouch for my good character.
- That's not necessary.

712
00:38:11.582 --> 00:38:12.822
Oh, but it is, though.

713
00:38:14.210 --> 00:38:17.690
I thought you ought to know something about my life.
I didn't want you to think I was...

714
00:38:17.755 --> 00:38:18.506
Well...

715
00:38:18.714 --> 00:38:20.594
...I didn't want you to think
I was some nobody.

716
00:38:21.217 --> 00:38:25.221
You see, old sport, I'm going to make
a very big request of you today.

717
00:38:25.721 --> 00:38:26.931
A big request?

718
00:38:27.139 --> 00:38:27.932
Yes.

719
00:38:28.140 --> 00:38:29.934
Miss Baker will explain everything...

720
00:38:30.142 --> 00:38:31.268
...when you take her to tea.

721
00:38:33.103 --> 00:38:35.147
Jordan? What's she got to do with it?

722
00:38:36.148 --> 00:38:36.988
Well, I assure you...

723
00:38:37.107 --> 00:38:38.147
...it's nothing underhand.

724
00:38:38.317 --> 00:38:40.528
Miss Baker's an honest sportswoman.
She wouldn't do...

725
00:38:40.694 --> 00:38:42.055
...anything that wasn't all right.

726
00:38:42.488 --> 00:38:43.155
Pull over!

727
00:38:43.322 --> 00:38:46.659
- Pull over to the curb!
- All right, old sport. All right.

728
00:38:52.081 --> 00:38:52.873
Right you are!

729
00:38:53.082 --> 00:38:54.122
I'll know you next time...

730
00:38:54.124 --> 00:38:55.042
...Mr. Gatsby!

731
00:38:55.209 --> 00:38:57.503
- Excuse me.
- Thank you.

732
00:38:59.338 --> 00:39:00.673
One of your old Oxford pals?

733
00:39:00.881 --> 00:39:04.343
Well, I was able to do the commissioner
a favor once.

734
00:39:04.552 --> 00:39:06.387
He sends me a Christmas card every year.

735
00:39:07.930 --> 00:39:10.140
I imagine he'll be at lunch too. Heh.

736
00:39:15.187 --> 00:39:16.027
<i>By the time...</i>

737
00:39:16.063 --> 00:39:18.440
<i>...we reached the bridge,
I was impossibly confused.</i>

738
00:39:30.911 --> 00:39:33.163
<i>I didn't know what to think.</i>

739
00:39:33.873 --> 00:39:36.584
<i>But the city seen
from the Queensboro Bridge...</i>

740
00:39:36.750 --> 00:39:40.880
<i>...is always the city seen
for the first time...</i>

741
00:39:41.130 --> 00:39:44.925
<i>...in its first wild promise
of all the mystery...</i>

742
00:39:45.134 --> 00:39:46.135
<i>...and the beauty...</i>

743
00:39:46.343 --> 00:39:48.137
<i>...in the world.</i>

744
00:39:48.554 --> 00:39:49.221
<i>Anything...</i>

745
00:39:49.430 --> 00:39:52.224
<i>...can happen now that we've slid over...</i>

746
00:39:52.433 --> 00:39:53.642
<i>...this bridge, I thought.</i>

747
00:39:54.101 --> 00:39:55.561
<i>Anything at all.</i>

748
00:39:55.769 --> 00:39:58.105
<i>Even Gatsby could happen.</i>

749
00:40:00.107 --> 00:40:02.234
Yes, absolutely.

750
00:40:03.611 --> 00:40:04.403
Oh!

751
00:40:04.570 --> 00:40:06.030
Ah!

752
00:40:07.114 --> 00:40:08.824
My boy!

753
00:40:09.199 --> 00:40:10.359
Meyer, Meyer, Meyer.

754
00:40:10.784 --> 00:40:12.453
Mm. Smell so good.

755
00:40:12.661 --> 00:40:14.022
- Look at you!
- Look at you.

756
00:40:14.079 --> 00:40:16.832
Mr. Carraway, this is my good friend,
Mr. Meyer Wolfshiem.

757
00:40:17.041 --> 00:40:18.083
A wonderful pleasure...

758
00:40:18.292 --> 00:40:19.668
- ...Mr. Carraway.
- My pleasure.

759
00:40:19.877 --> 00:40:21.211
I know all about you.

760
00:40:21.545 --> 00:40:23.213
- I see.
- Yes! Mr. Gatsby's...

761
00:40:23.380 --> 00:40:25.020
- ...always talking about you.
- Really?

762
00:40:26.717 --> 00:40:27.509
Shall we?

763
00:40:28.552 --> 00:40:29.553
Come.

764
00:40:31.221 --> 00:40:32.142
Join us for a little...

765
00:40:32.181 --> 00:40:33.557
...lunch.

766
00:40:51.075 --> 00:40:54.119
<i>Hundred, hundred, hundred dollar bills</i>

767
00:40:57.039 --> 00:40:58.290
Yeah!

768
00:40:58.499 --> 00:40:59.979
- Hands off!
- Out! Out you go!

769
00:41:00.167 --> 00:41:00.927
Get off me!

770
00:41:01.585 --> 00:41:04.213
Tell Walter Chase he
keeps his mouth shut...

771
00:41:04.421 --> 00:41:05.581
...or he doesn't get a penny.

772
00:41:05.798 --> 00:41:06.879
We'll talk about...

773
00:41:06.882 --> 00:41:09.051
- ...that later.
- Highballs, Mr. Gatsby?

774
00:41:09.259 --> 00:41:10.219
Highballs it is.

775
00:41:10.427 --> 00:41:12.507
- All right.
- You take care of my friend.

776
00:41:13.639 --> 00:41:15.140
Look who's here.

777
00:41:15.349 --> 00:41:16.509
You see these fists?

778
00:41:16.517 --> 00:41:17.851
He's the next heavyweight...

779
00:41:18.060 --> 00:41:19.895
- ...champion.
- Pay my respects to your boss.

780
00:41:20.437 --> 00:41:21.522
Hey, Jay!

781
00:41:21.730 --> 00:41:23.357
You're under arrest!
Ha-ha-ha!

782
00:41:23.565 --> 00:41:27.778
You be careful, now. You're turning
into a real jazz hound, commissioner.

783
00:41:27.945 --> 00:41:29.488
Bang, bang!

784
00:41:31.115 --> 00:41:33.075
- That's the commissioner.
- Mr. Gatsby...

785
00:41:33.242 --> 00:41:34.576
...your table is ready.

786
00:41:34.785 --> 00:41:36.704
- Gatsby!
- Good to see you.

787
00:41:37.079 --> 00:41:38.497
Yeah, that's fantastic.

788
00:41:39.832 --> 00:41:41.625
You be careful at those tables now...

789
00:41:41.834 --> 00:41:43.085
...senator.

790
00:41:43.293 --> 00:41:44.837
I'll put a bet on for you, Jay!

791
00:41:45.004 --> 00:41:46.505
<i>Hundred dollar bills</i>

792
00:41:50.217 --> 00:41:51.260
We'll have the lobster.

793
00:41:51.468 --> 00:41:53.470
It's decorated with
truffles and fine herbs.

794
00:42:01.061 --> 00:42:02.271
<i>Hundred dollar bills</i>

795
00:42:02.980 --> 00:42:03.661
So...

796
00:42:03.814 --> 00:42:06.525
...how is the bond business,
Mr. Carraway?

797
00:42:07.609 --> 00:42:08.652
Fine. Thank you.

798
00:42:08.861 --> 00:42:10.237
I understand you're looking for...

799
00:42:10.446 --> 00:42:12.656
- ...a business connection.
- No. No, no, no.

800
00:42:12.823 --> 00:42:13.532
No, no, no.

801
00:42:13.699 --> 00:42:14.908
This isn't the man, Meyer.

802
00:42:15.325 --> 00:42:16.410
This is the friend...

803
00:42:16.618 --> 00:42:18.203
...that I told you about.

804
00:42:18.412 --> 00:42:20.164
Oh, I beg your pardon.

805
00:42:20.372 --> 00:42:21.415
I had a wrong man.

806
00:42:23.834 --> 00:42:25.354
If you'll excuse me, I have to make...

807
00:42:25.586 --> 00:42:26.628
...that call.

808
00:42:30.382 --> 00:42:31.102
Any luck, senator?

809
00:42:31.258 --> 00:42:32.551
What a gentleman.

810
00:42:32.760 --> 00:42:34.928
From one of the finest families
in the Midwest.

811
00:42:36.305 --> 00:42:37.723
Sadly, all dead now.

812
00:42:38.432 --> 00:42:43.479
When I first made the pleasure of Mr. Gatsby's
acquaintance just after the war...

813
00:42:44.063 --> 00:42:47.316
...I knew I'd discovered
a man of fine breeding.

814
00:42:47.524 --> 00:42:48.358
A war hero.

815
00:42:48.567 --> 00:42:49.985
Such medals.

816
00:42:50.778 --> 00:42:51.695
And...

817
00:42:52.196 --> 00:42:53.113
And...

818
00:42:53.280 --> 00:42:54.531
...an Oxford man.

819
00:42:55.115 --> 00:42:56.241
You know Oxford?

820
00:42:56.450 --> 00:42:58.202
Yes, I've heard of it.

821
00:42:58.410 --> 00:43:00.970
Then you'd know
that when it comes to married women...

822
00:43:01.038 --> 00:43:02.414
...a man like this can be trusted.

823
00:43:03.373 --> 00:43:04.625
With a friend...

824
00:43:04.833 --> 00:43:06.126
...with someone like you...

825
00:43:06.460 --> 00:43:09.671
...he'd never so much as look at your wife.

826
00:43:10.881 --> 00:43:12.007
I'm not married.

827
00:43:12.216 --> 00:43:14.510
But you work on Wall Street, right?

828
00:43:14.843 --> 00:43:15.886
Yes.

829
00:43:16.220 --> 00:43:16.970
Ah.

830
00:43:18.555 --> 00:43:20.099
Looking at my tiepin?

831
00:43:20.307 --> 00:43:21.809
Finest specimen of human...

832
00:43:22.017 --> 00:43:23.102
...molar.

833
00:43:23.268 --> 00:43:24.028
Gentlemen.

834
00:43:25.479 --> 00:43:26.480
Everything all right?

835
00:43:26.897 --> 00:43:28.941
Yes! Yes. We...

836
00:43:29.149 --> 00:43:31.777
We were just talking about
other people's wives.

837
00:43:31.985 --> 00:43:33.112
Other people's wives?

838
00:43:33.320 --> 00:43:34.279
- Yes.
- Really?

839
00:43:34.446 --> 00:43:37.157
Well, my work here is done.
I'm going to leave you gentlemen...

840
00:43:37.366 --> 00:43:39.409
...to talk about your sports
and your women.

841
00:43:40.119 --> 00:43:41.120
Other people's wives.

842
00:43:41.328 --> 00:43:43.038
Hello, ladies.

843
00:43:43.539 --> 00:43:45.040
Ooh.

844
00:43:45.958 --> 00:43:46.958
Who is he, anyhow?

845
00:43:47.084 --> 00:43:47.751
An actor?

846
00:43:47.960 --> 00:43:48.877
Meyer?

847
00:43:49.086 --> 00:43:51.046
No, no. He's a gambler.

848
00:43:53.757 --> 00:43:56.135
He's the man who fixed
the 1919 World Series.

849
00:43:57.261 --> 00:43:58.095
Fixed it?

850
00:43:58.303 --> 00:43:58.971
Fixed it.

851
00:43:59.179 --> 00:44:00.430
Well, how'd he manage that?

852
00:44:01.640 --> 00:44:03.600
Saw the opportunity, I suppose.

853
00:44:04.268 --> 00:44:06.019
He's a very smart man.

854
00:44:15.946 --> 00:44:19.575
Now, old sport, about Miss Baker
and that request we spoke of.

855
00:44:19.783 --> 00:44:22.161
Nick? Nick!

856
00:44:22.369 --> 00:44:24.037
Tom! Funny seeing you!

857
00:44:24.246 --> 00:44:26.039
- How have you been?
- Yeah.

858
00:44:26.248 --> 00:44:28.542
- Good.
- Nick, Daisy is furious...

859
00:44:28.750 --> 00:44:30.586
- ...that you haven't called.
- Hey...

860
00:44:30.794 --> 00:44:33.630
...Mr. Gatsby, this is Mr. Buchanan.

861
00:44:35.257 --> 00:44:37.217
It's a pleasure to make your acquaintance.

862
00:44:37.759 --> 00:44:38.552
Yeah.

863
00:44:39.553 --> 00:44:41.889
Never would have expected
to find you in this temple...

864
00:44:42.097 --> 00:44:43.698
- ...of virtue.
- I was having lunch...

865
00:44:43.724 --> 00:44:45.017
...with Mr. Gatsby.

866
00:44:53.734 --> 00:44:55.444
- Can I help you, sir?
- Jordan Baker...

867
00:44:55.652 --> 00:44:58.363
...the famous sporting star?
The golfer? I see.

868
00:44:58.572 --> 00:44:59.990
I've spotted her. Thank you.

869
00:45:00.199 --> 00:45:01.033
There you are.

870
00:45:01.241 --> 00:45:02.242
What game are you and Gatsby...

871
00:45:02.409 --> 00:45:03.076
...playing at?

872
00:45:03.243 --> 00:45:04.494
- The gentleman will be joining...?
- Excuse me.

873
00:45:04.703 --> 00:45:05.829
- Nick, please.
- It's all rather...

874
00:45:05.996 --> 00:45:07.756
...strange. He picks me
up in his fancy car.

875
00:45:07.915 --> 00:45:08.916
- Going on about...
- Keep your...

876
00:45:09.082 --> 00:45:10.000
- ...his life.
- ...voice down.

877
00:45:10.167 --> 00:45:10.968
People can hear you.

878
00:45:11.001 --> 00:45:11.668
What is...

879
00:45:11.835 --> 00:45:13.420
...this enormous request?

880
00:45:13.629 --> 00:45:15.839
He wants you to invite Daisy to tea.

881
00:45:16.882 --> 00:45:18.050
Daisy?

882
00:45:19.885 --> 00:45:21.386
And Gatsby.

883
00:45:22.429 --> 00:45:23.555
Why?

884
00:45:23.764 --> 00:45:25.766
I don't quite know where to start.

885
00:45:26.099 --> 00:45:29.144
You see, I didn't realize
until the other night that...

886
00:45:29.353 --> 00:45:30.854
...I'd met Gatsby.

887
00:45:31.563 --> 00:45:34.691
Five years ago. In Louisville.

888
00:45:35.609 --> 00:45:38.320
<i>It was the day I got
my new English golf shoes.</i>

889
00:45:42.157 --> 00:45:42.824
<i>Daisy...</i>

890
00:45:42.991 --> 00:45:45.452
<i>...was by far the most popular girl with...</i>

891
00:45:45.661 --> 00:45:46.982
<i>...the officers from Camp Taylor.</i>

892
00:45:47.287 --> 00:45:48.747
Hello, Jordan.

893
00:45:49.248 --> 00:45:49.998
<i>One of them was...</i>

894
00:45:50.207 --> 00:45:51.333
<i>...in the car with her.</i>

895
00:45:51.541 --> 00:45:53.752
<i>It was Gatsby.</i>

896
00:45:53.961 --> 00:45:55.754
<i>The way he looked at her is the way...</i>

897
00:45:55.963 --> 00:45:57.714
<i>...all girls want to be looked at.</i>

898
00:45:58.465 --> 00:45:59.424
So...

899
00:45:59.591 --> 00:46:00.759
...tell me what happened.

900
00:46:01.134 --> 00:46:02.469
Well, I don't know.

901
00:46:02.678 --> 00:46:03.720
Charge!

902
00:46:06.932 --> 00:46:08.132
<i>Gatsby was sent off...</i>

903
00:46:08.141 --> 00:46:08.809
<i>...to war.</i>

904
00:46:09.851 --> 00:46:10.936
<i>When the war ended...</i>

905
00:46:11.353 --> 00:46:12.396
<i>...Daisy waited.</i>

906
00:46:12.604 --> 00:46:16.817
<i>But for some unknown reason,
Gatsby couldn't return.</i>

907
00:46:17.317 --> 00:46:22.447
<i>A year later, Tom Buchanan of Chicago
swept in and stole her away.</i>

908
00:46:22.739 --> 00:46:25.701
<i>He gave her a string of pearls
worth $350,000.</i>

909
00:46:28.620 --> 00:46:30.205
<i>But the morning of the wedding...</i>

910
00:46:30.414 --> 00:46:32.334
<i>- ...Daisy received a letter.</i>
<i>- Tell them...</i>

911
00:46:32.416 --> 00:46:33.583
<i>...Daisy's changed her mind!</i>

912
00:46:33.834 --> 00:46:35.874
- What is this?
- Daisy, please!

913
00:46:36.169 --> 00:46:37.504
- Hand it to me!
- No!

914
00:46:38.088 --> 00:46:39.047
Leave me alone!

915
00:46:39.256 --> 00:46:41.376
Jordan, no one must know about this.

916
00:46:41.550 --> 00:46:43.260
<i>- What was in the letter?</i>
<i>- I don't know.</i>

917
00:46:43.468 --> 00:46:45.053
<i>She wouldn't tell me.</i>

918
00:46:45.679 --> 00:46:46.888
<i>But it was too late.</i>

919
00:46:47.889 --> 00:46:50.309
<i>That day at 5:00, Daisy Fay...</i>

920
00:46:50.517 --> 00:46:51.557
<i>...married Tom Buchanan...</i>

921
00:46:51.601 --> 00:46:54.161
<i>...with more pomp and circumstance
than Louisville had ever seen.</i>

922
00:46:54.896 --> 00:46:56.606
Congratulations, Mr. Buchanan!

923
00:47:00.319 --> 00:47:03.697
After the honeymoon,
I saw them in Santa Barbara.

924
00:47:03.905 --> 00:47:08.076
Well, it was touching, really. I'd never
seen a girl so in love with her husband.

925
00:47:08.285 --> 00:47:09.494
A week later, Tom...

926
00:47:09.703 --> 00:47:11.079
...crashed his car.

927
00:47:11.288 --> 00:47:13.415
The girl with him was a chambermaid
at the Santa Barbara Hotel.

928
00:47:13.707 --> 00:47:15.792
It got into all the papers.

929
00:47:16.918 --> 00:47:19.546
- It's a strange coincidence.
- What is?

930
00:47:19.755 --> 00:47:22.632
The fact that Gatsby's house
is just across the bay.

931
00:47:23.592 --> 00:47:24.843
It's no coincidence.

932
00:47:25.344 --> 00:47:28.055
He bought that house to be near her.

933
00:47:28.263 --> 00:47:31.683
He threw all those parties
hoping she'd wander in one night.

934
00:47:31.892 --> 00:47:36.313
He constantly asked about Daisy.
I was just the first person that knew her.

935
00:47:37.439 --> 00:47:40.067
All that for a girl
he hasn't seen in five years.

936
00:47:40.400 --> 00:47:42.944
And now he just wants me
to invite her over to tea.

937
00:47:43.779 --> 00:47:45.405
The modesty of it.

938
00:47:45.864 --> 00:47:46.990
Kind of takes your breath...

939
00:47:47.157 --> 00:47:49.785
- ...away, doesn't it?
- Evening, sweethearts. Where are...

940
00:47:49.951 --> 00:47:50.786
...you kids going?

941
00:47:50.994 --> 00:47:52.996
- Long Island, please.
- And you think...

942
00:47:53.163 --> 00:47:56.375
...I should. I mean, does Daisy
want to see Gatsby?

943
00:47:56.583 --> 00:47:58.126
She's absolutely not to know.

944
00:47:58.335 --> 00:48:00.045
You're supposed to invite her over...

945
00:48:00.253 --> 00:48:01.713
...so he can happen to pass by.

946
00:48:02.631 --> 00:48:05.133
<i>I remember feeling torn.</i>

947
00:48:05.342 --> 00:48:09.137
<i>Was it right to bring my cousin Daisy,
a married woman...</i>

948
00:48:09.346 --> 00:48:12.057
<i>...together with a man I hardly knew?</i>

949
00:48:17.396 --> 00:48:21.316
<i>When I returned home,
Gatsby's was lit from tower to cellar...</i>

950
00:48:21.483 --> 00:48:23.944
<i>...as if for another wild party.</i>

951
00:48:28.073 --> 00:48:30.367
<i>But there wasn't a sound.</i>

952
00:48:44.548 --> 00:48:46.428
- Thank you.
- Have a good night.

953
00:48:56.101 --> 00:48:58.061
Your place looks like the World's Fair...

954
00:48:58.270 --> 00:49:00.272
...or Coney Island.

955
00:49:00.480 --> 00:49:02.232
- Does it?
- Yes.

956
00:49:02.441 --> 00:49:05.110
Well, I've just been glancing...

957
00:49:05.277 --> 00:49:07.195
...into some of the rooms.
What do you say we...

958
00:49:07.404 --> 00:49:09.698
...go to Coney Island?
We could take my car.

959
00:49:09.865 --> 00:49:11.908
Oh, uh, it's too late tonight.

960
00:49:12.117 --> 00:49:14.957
We could take a plunge in the pool.
I haven't made use of it all summer.

961
00:49:15.120 --> 00:49:16.538
I must go to bed.

962
00:49:17.789 --> 00:49:18.790
All right.

963
00:49:26.923 --> 00:49:28.008
Happy to do it.

964
00:49:29.468 --> 00:49:30.844
I'm going to call Daisy and...

965
00:49:31.052 --> 00:49:33.013
-...invite her to tea.
- That's all right...

966
00:49:33.221 --> 00:49:35.015
- What day would suit you?
- What day would suit you?

967
00:49:35.223 --> 00:49:37.058
I don't want to put you to any trouble.

968
00:49:37.267 --> 00:49:38.602
Day after tomorrow all right?

969
00:49:39.269 --> 00:49:40.937
Day after tomorrow?

970
00:49:41.771 --> 00:49:42.772
Well, I... I'd...

971
00:49:44.566 --> 00:49:46.318
See, I'd want to get the grass cut.

972
00:49:49.404 --> 00:49:50.238
Uh...

973
00:49:52.240 --> 00:49:53.617
Well, look here, old sport.

974
00:49:53.825 --> 00:49:55.410
You don't make much money, do you?

975
00:49:55.911 --> 00:49:57.621
- Not really.
- If you'll forgive me...

976
00:49:58.788 --> 00:50:03.543
You see, I happen to run a little business
on the side. A sort of sideline.

977
00:50:03.877 --> 00:50:06.505
You understand what I'm saying, right?
You do sell bonds...

978
00:50:06.713 --> 00:50:07.839
...don't you, old sport?

979
00:50:08.048 --> 00:50:09.132
- I'm trying to.
- Right.

980
00:50:09.341 --> 00:50:10.884
Well, happens to be a rather...

981
00:50:11.051 --> 00:50:12.093
...confidential sort of thing...

982
00:50:12.302 --> 00:50:13.720
...but you might make
a nice bit of money.

983
00:50:13.929 --> 00:50:16.570
- No, thank you. I have my hands full.
- You wouldn't have to do...

984
00:50:16.681 --> 00:50:18.183
...any business with Wolfshiem,
I assure you.

985
00:50:18.391 --> 00:50:20.477
It's a favor, Jay. Just a favor.

986
00:50:23.188 --> 00:50:25.524
Yes. Happy to do it.

987
00:50:26.107 --> 00:50:27.692
- Favor?
- Yes.

988
00:50:29.361 --> 00:50:30.487
Hm.

989
00:50:32.864 --> 00:50:33.657
Well, good night.

990
00:50:33.865 --> 00:50:35.450
Good night.

991
00:50:53.885 --> 00:50:55.303
Good morning.

992
00:51:23.498 --> 00:51:26.459
One of the papers says they thought
the rain would stop about 4.

993
00:51:28.336 --> 00:51:31.047
I think it was the...
I think it was the Journal.

994
00:51:31.214 --> 00:51:32.424
Ah.

995
00:51:34.342 --> 00:51:36.222
Just in to the right, gentlemen.
To the right...

996
00:51:36.344 --> 00:51:37.721
...in the living room. Thank you.

997
00:51:43.184 --> 00:51:44.978
Is everything all right?

998
00:51:45.186 --> 00:51:47.226
The grass looks fine,
if that's what you mean.

999
00:51:47.897 --> 00:51:48.565
Grass?

1000
00:51:49.024 --> 00:51:50.900
What grass?

1001
00:51:54.487 --> 00:51:56.156
I bought cakes.

1002
00:52:07.542 --> 00:52:08.209
Have you...

1003
00:52:08.418 --> 00:52:09.739
Have you got everything you need?

1004
00:52:10.170 --> 00:52:11.630
Perhaps more flowers.

1005
00:52:12.964 --> 00:52:14.883
I think they did a fine job, don't you?

1006
00:52:15.091 --> 00:52:16.343
Beautiful.

1007
00:52:16.718 --> 00:52:18.303
You think it's too much?

1008
00:52:18.845 --> 00:52:21.473
Uh, I think it's what you want.

1009
00:52:22.015 --> 00:52:23.767
I think so too.

1010
00:52:45.664 --> 00:52:46.331
I can't...

1011
00:52:46.539 --> 00:52:47.791
...wait all day.

1012
00:52:48.249 --> 00:52:49.292
I'm leaving.

1013
00:52:49.501 --> 00:52:51.252
Don't be silly. It's two minutes to 4.

1014
00:52:51.461 --> 00:52:52.861
No one's coming to tea! It's too...

1015
00:53:02.013 --> 00:53:03.264
It's her!

1016
00:53:17.529 --> 00:53:20.156
Is this absolutely where you live,
my dearest one?

1017
00:53:20.365 --> 00:53:21.325
<i>Yes, it suits me.</i>

1018
00:53:22.325 --> 00:53:23.325
<i>Why did I have...</i>

1019
00:53:23.368 --> 00:53:25.829
...to come alone? Are you in love with me?

1020
00:53:26.037 --> 00:53:28.123
<i>Oh, it's the secret
of Carraway Castle.</i>

1021
00:53:28.331 --> 00:53:31.084
- Tell your chauffeur to go far away.
- Come back in an hour...

1022
00:53:31.292 --> 00:53:32.419
...Ferdie.

1023
00:53:32.627 --> 00:53:33.962
His name is Ferdie.

1024
00:53:41.636 --> 00:53:42.804
Oh.

1025
00:53:46.307 --> 00:53:49.602
Oh. My goodness.

1026
00:53:49.811 --> 00:53:52.147
I can't believe it.

1027
00:53:53.398 --> 00:53:57.110
You... Did you ransack a greenhouse?

1028
00:53:58.069 --> 00:54:00.238
He is in love with me.

1029
00:54:14.377 --> 00:54:15.044
Would you?

1030
00:54:16.045 --> 00:54:17.005
That's funny.

1031
00:54:17.213 --> 00:54:18.506
What's funny?

1032
00:54:22.385 --> 00:54:23.386
Ah.

1033
00:54:27.557 --> 00:54:28.475
What are you doing?

1034
00:55:19.651 --> 00:55:22.403
I'm certainly glad to see you again.

1035
00:55:24.739 --> 00:55:27.492
Hi. I'm...

1036
00:55:27.659 --> 00:55:30.453
I'm certainly glad to see you, as well.

1037
00:55:58.606 --> 00:56:00.525
We've... We've...

1038
00:56:01.442 --> 00:56:02.318
We've met before.

1039
00:56:06.155 --> 00:56:08.491
I'll have someone come repair this...

1040
00:56:08.700 --> 00:56:09.909
...immediately.

1041
00:56:26.217 --> 00:56:28.052
Sorry about the clock.

1042
00:56:28.928 --> 00:56:30.722
- That's an old clock.
- Lovely, though.

1043
00:56:30.930 --> 00:56:32.390
- A lovely clock.
- Yes.

1044
00:56:34.559 --> 00:56:36.060
We haven't met for many years.

1045
00:56:36.269 --> 00:56:37.896
Five years next November.

1046
00:56:40.857 --> 00:56:41.816
Tea?

1047
00:56:42.025 --> 00:56:43.943
- Yes, thank you, old sport.
- Thank you.

1048
00:56:44.193 --> 00:56:45.778
- Lemon or sugar?
- Neither.

1049
00:56:46.029 --> 00:56:47.530
Plain. Thank you.

1050
00:57:06.215 --> 00:57:07.216
I just have to pop...

1051
00:57:07.383 --> 00:57:08.301
- ...into town.
- Town?

1052
00:57:08.509 --> 00:57:09.385
I'll be right back.

1053
00:57:09.594 --> 00:57:10.553
Nick, I've...

1054
00:57:10.720 --> 00:57:11.600
...gotta speak to you.

1055
00:57:11.679 --> 00:57:14.182
- Yes. I'll be right back.
- God.

1056
00:57:14.390 --> 00:57:17.018
This was a mistake.
This was a terrible, terrible mistake.

1057
00:57:17.226 --> 00:57:19.146
You're just embarrassed.
Daisy's embarrassed too.

1058
00:57:21.773 --> 00:57:23.441
- She's embarrassed?
- Yes.

1059
00:57:23.650 --> 00:57:24.400
Just as much as you are.

1060
00:57:24.609 --> 00:57:25.860
- Don't talk so loud.
- Hey.

1061
00:57:26.069 --> 00:57:28.905
You're acting like a little boy.
You're being rude. Daisy's...

1062
00:57:29.113 --> 00:57:30.865
-...in there all alone and you're...
- Shh.

1063
00:58:06.317 --> 00:58:08.820
<i>Looking over my story so far...</i>

1064
00:58:08.987 --> 00:58:13.324
<i>...I'm reminded that
for the second time that summer...</i>

1065
00:58:13.491 --> 00:58:16.619
<i>...I was guarding other people's secrets.</i>

1066
00:58:17.870 --> 00:58:20.873
<i>Once again, I was within...</i>

1067
00:58:21.582 --> 00:58:23.209
<i>...and without.</i>

1068
00:58:48.359 --> 00:58:49.610
Ahem!

1069
00:58:51.446 --> 00:58:52.989
It's stopped raining.

1070
00:58:56.826 --> 00:58:57.952
Yes.

1071
00:58:58.327 --> 00:58:59.787
It has, hasn't it?

1072
00:59:12.216 --> 00:59:14.093
What do you think of that, Daisy?

1073
00:59:16.345 --> 00:59:17.388
Come look.

1074
00:59:17.889 --> 00:59:20.016
Oh, Nicky. How funny.

1075
00:59:21.642 --> 00:59:23.853
Look, it's my house.

1076
00:59:26.773 --> 00:59:28.858
Just there across the bay.

1077
00:59:35.031 --> 00:59:36.365
I know.

1078
00:59:38.034 --> 00:59:40.620
I have the same view from my place.

1079
00:59:42.205 --> 00:59:43.331
Where's your place?

1080
00:59:46.417 --> 00:59:47.085
Nick...

1081
00:59:48.586 --> 00:59:52.215
...I want you and Daisy to come over
to my house. I'd like to show her around.

1082
00:59:52.423 --> 00:59:53.883
You sure you want me to come?

1083
00:59:54.092 --> 00:59:56.260
Absolutely, old sport.

1084
00:59:56.719 --> 00:59:58.387
Absolutely.

1085
00:59:59.931 --> 01:00:01.349
Ladner, open the gates.

1086
01:00:01.557 --> 01:00:02.558
Open the gates!

1087
01:00:02.767 --> 01:00:05.186
I had the gates brought in
from a castle in Normandy.

1088
01:00:08.106 --> 01:00:08.773
Oh, Jay.

1089
01:00:08.981 --> 01:00:10.691
- It's so grand.
- You like it?

1090
01:00:12.819 --> 01:00:13.486
I love it.

1091
01:00:14.737 --> 01:00:16.030
But how do you live here...

1092
01:00:16.239 --> 01:00:18.157
- ...all alone?
- Well, I don't.

1093
01:00:18.366 --> 01:00:22.703
I keep it always full of
interesting, celebrated people.

1094
01:00:23.746 --> 01:00:25.206
Come with me.

1095
01:00:30.211 --> 01:00:31.796
The house looks well, doesn't it?

1096
01:00:32.004 --> 01:00:34.205
See the way the whole front
catches the light like that?

1097
01:00:34.215 --> 01:00:35.716
Oh, it's splendid.

1098
01:00:39.137 --> 01:00:40.680
Come on, you two. I want...

1099
01:00:40.847 --> 01:00:41.567
...the royal tour.

1100
01:00:41.681 --> 01:00:44.433
You must understand that I like all things
that are modern. Presses the orange...

1101
01:00:44.642 --> 01:00:46.477
...it all comes out there.

1102
01:00:53.901 --> 01:00:55.403
Yay!

1103
01:00:55.945 --> 01:00:57.385
Anyone for a round of golf?

1104
01:00:57.405 --> 01:01:00.867
- You do know I'm a champion golfer?
- Great!

1105
01:01:01.075 --> 01:01:02.201
Well, go on.

1106
01:01:02.368 --> 01:01:03.208
I thought we could...

1107
01:01:03.452 --> 01:01:05.332
Remarkable little camera.
Latest design.

1108
01:01:05.538 --> 01:01:06.978
I'll show you how it's done.

1109
01:01:07.165 --> 01:01:08.486
- Nick, keep filming.
- All right.

1110
01:01:08.666 --> 01:01:09.542
- She's gonna take a swing.
- I'm ready.

1111
01:01:09.750 --> 01:01:11.127
Aim. Fore!

1112
01:01:14.088 --> 01:01:15.088
I'm such a brute.

1113
01:01:15.256 --> 01:01:17.341
- You ready for your close-up?
- I don't want to forget...

1114
01:01:17.508 --> 01:01:18.593
- ...a single moment.
- You're shimmering.

1115
01:01:18.801 --> 01:01:20.761
She looks like she could be
on the cover of Vogue.

1116
01:01:20.928 --> 01:01:22.009
Turn on the camera!

1117
01:01:23.973 --> 01:01:26.267
- Where did you go?
- Slow down.

1118
01:01:26.893 --> 01:01:27.894
Slow down.

1119
01:01:32.481 --> 01:01:33.816
Ahh.

1120
01:01:34.400 --> 01:01:35.067
It's beautiful.

1121
01:01:35.651 --> 01:01:37.731
- It's a custom Wurlitzer.
- Can anyone...

1122
01:01:37.737 --> 01:01:38.779
...play that?

1123
01:01:38.946 --> 01:01:40.426
Klipspringer can. Someone wake Ewing.

1124
01:01:40.531 --> 01:01:42.617
- Of course, sir.
- Music!

1125
01:01:42.825 --> 01:01:43.785
And then we can dance...

1126
01:01:43.826 --> 01:01:46.162
- ...all night.
- Ewing's a genius, plays anything.

1127
01:01:46.579 --> 01:01:49.099
- Then I demand a Charleston.
- She makes it look so splendid...

1128
01:01:49.290 --> 01:01:50.917
...don't you think, old sport?

1129
01:01:53.294 --> 01:01:54.694
I have a man in England...

1130
01:01:54.712 --> 01:01:56.130
...who buys me clothes.

1131
01:01:56.422 --> 01:01:58.062
I've never seen anything like it.

1132
01:01:58.174 --> 01:01:59.217
Something for the lady.

1133
01:02:00.218 --> 01:02:01.719
He sends over a selection at...

1134
01:02:01.928 --> 01:02:03.208
...the beginning of each season.

1135
01:02:04.805 --> 01:02:05.556
These are silk.

1136
01:02:05.765 --> 01:02:07.600
- Jay!
- These are flannel.

1137
01:02:07.767 --> 01:02:09.352
They're so beautiful!

1138
01:02:09.560 --> 01:02:10.481
Indian cotton.

1139
01:02:10.519 --> 01:02:12.146
Stop it. Jay!

1140
01:02:12.355 --> 01:02:13.606
Linen.

1141
01:02:14.023 --> 01:02:14.690
Nicky...

1142
01:02:14.857 --> 01:02:16.458
- ...he's a madman!
- I can't help you!

1143
01:02:16.734 --> 01:02:17.415
Flannel!

1144
01:02:17.652 --> 01:02:18.973
You're going to have to refold...

1145
01:02:19.111 --> 01:02:20.232
...every single one of them.

1146
01:02:21.239 --> 01:02:22.031
You'll ruin them!

1147
01:02:26.035 --> 01:02:26.827
Jay! Stop it.

1148
01:02:27.870 --> 01:02:29.030
- Right here!
- No!

1149
01:02:29.205 --> 01:02:30.289
Jay!

1150
01:02:47.223 --> 01:02:48.307
What is it?

1151
01:02:50.977 --> 01:02:53.729
Daisy, Daisy, darling, what is it?

1152
01:02:58.609 --> 01:03:00.444
It makes me sad.

1153
01:03:02.655 --> 01:03:03.489
Why?

1154
01:03:03.948 --> 01:03:05.491
Because...

1155
01:03:09.078 --> 01:03:11.205
<i>Five lost years...</i>

1156
01:03:11.414 --> 01:03:13.874
<i>...struggled on Daisy's lips.</i>

1157
01:03:15.126 --> 01:03:18.004
<i>But all she could manage was:</i>

1158
01:03:20.464 --> 01:03:21.757
Why?

1159
01:03:23.968 --> 01:03:26.721
Because I've never seen
such beautiful shirts before.

1160
01:03:48.367 --> 01:03:50.619
If it wasn't for the mist...

1161
01:03:51.203 --> 01:03:53.497
...we could see the green light.

1162
01:03:59.086 --> 01:04:00.963
What green light?

1163
01:04:02.173 --> 01:04:03.716
The one that burns all night...

1164
01:04:03.924 --> 01:04:05.801
...at the end of your dock.

1165
01:04:09.013 --> 01:04:09.722
<i>Possibly...</i>

1166
01:04:09.930 --> 01:04:12.099
<i>...it had occurred to Gatsby that...</i>

1167
01:04:12.308 --> 01:04:14.518
<i>...the colossal significance
of that light...</i>

1168
01:04:14.727 --> 01:04:16.771
<i>...had vanished forever.</i>

1169
01:04:17.605 --> 01:04:19.648
<i>Now it was once again...</i>

1170
01:04:19.857 --> 01:04:21.137
<i>...just a green light on a dock.</i>

1171
01:04:21.150 --> 01:04:25.571
<i>And his count of enchanted objects
had diminished by one.</i>

1172
01:04:27.448 --> 01:04:28.115
Who's this?

1173
01:04:28.324 --> 01:04:29.700
- Your father?
- Oh, no.

1174
01:04:29.909 --> 01:04:31.702
That's Mr. Dan Cody, old sport.

1175
01:04:33.079 --> 01:04:34.413
He's dead now.

1176
01:04:34.622 --> 01:04:37.041
He used to be my best friend
many years ago.

1177
01:04:37.958 --> 01:04:39.079
You never told me...

1178
01:04:39.085 --> 01:04:40.920
...you had a pompadour. Or a yacht.

1179
01:04:41.379 --> 01:04:42.838
I wanna show you something.

1180
01:04:44.840 --> 01:04:46.258
Have a seat.

1181
01:04:46.967 --> 01:04:48.886
These are a lot of clippings I collected.

1182
01:04:50.262 --> 01:04:51.680
About you.

1183
01:04:55.142 --> 01:04:56.519
You saved my letters.

1184
01:04:58.062 --> 01:04:59.438
This was my first...

1185
01:04:59.647 --> 01:05:00.856
...photo of you.

1186
01:05:02.358 --> 01:05:03.025
You remember...

1187
01:05:03.192 --> 01:05:05.403
- ...this letter here?
<i>- We can't lose each other...</i>

1188
01:05:05.569 --> 01:05:06.612
<i>...and let all this...</i>

1189
01:05:06.779 --> 01:05:07.613
<i>glorious love...</i>

1190
01:05:07.780 --> 01:05:09.615
<i>...end in nothing. Come home.</i>

1191
01:05:10.324 --> 01:05:11.826
<i>I'll be here waiting and hoping...</i>

1192
01:05:12.743 --> 01:05:13.411
<i>...for every...</i>

1193
01:05:13.577 --> 01:05:15.788
<i>...long dream of you to come true.</i>

1194
01:05:19.750 --> 01:05:21.210
Excuse me.

1195
01:05:28.467 --> 01:05:32.388
<i>- Sir, Slagle's in Detroit.</i>
- I can't talk now, old sport.

1196
01:05:34.598 --> 01:05:37.560
He must know what a small town is.

1197
01:05:37.768 --> 01:05:39.895
No, you listen to me. Listen to me.

1198
01:05:43.607 --> 01:05:45.609
I said a small town.

1199
01:05:46.444 --> 01:05:47.611
He must know what a small...

1200
01:05:47.820 --> 01:05:48.612
...town is.

1201
01:05:52.741 --> 01:05:55.744
Listen to me, he's of no use to us
if Detroit is his idea...

1202
01:05:55.953 --> 01:05:57.872
...of a small town, you understand?

1203
01:06:03.627 --> 01:06:05.546
We'll chat later, old sport.

1204
01:06:06.714 --> 01:06:08.090
All right.

1205
01:06:11.802 --> 01:06:12.511
Klipspringer.

1206
01:06:14.680 --> 01:06:15.806
He must be awake.

1207
01:06:19.059 --> 01:06:20.269
Shall we?

1208
01:06:28.235 --> 01:06:29.195
Whoo!

1209
01:06:29.904 --> 01:06:30.863
Ho!

1210
01:07:12.071 --> 01:07:15.658
I wish I'd done everything
on earth with you.

1211
01:07:22.122 --> 01:07:23.832
All my life.

1212
01:07:26.627 --> 01:07:29.213
I wish it could always be like this.

1213
01:07:36.470 --> 01:07:38.264
It will be.

1214
01:07:42.184 --> 01:07:45.479
<i>If only it had been enough for Gatsby...</i>

1215
01:07:45.688 --> 01:07:47.523
<i>just to hold Daisy.</i>

1216
01:07:47.690 --> 01:07:52.570
<i>But he had a grand vision for his life
and Daisy's part in it.</i>

1217
01:07:54.196 --> 01:07:56.907
<i>It wasn't until the end of that summer...</i>

1218
01:07:57.116 --> 01:08:00.452
<i>...on the last night I saw Gatsby...</i>

1219
01:08:00.619 --> 01:08:04.206
<i>...that he told me of the life
he had dreamed for himself since...</i>

1220
01:08:04.415 --> 01:08:05.791
<i>...he was a boy.</i>

1221
01:08:06.000 --> 01:08:07.543
<i>You see, doctor...</i>

1222
01:08:08.210 --> 01:08:10.963
...Gatsby's real name was...

1223
01:08:12.423 --> 01:08:14.133
...James Gatz.

1224
01:08:15.175 --> 01:08:19.888
<i>His parents were dirt-poor farmers
from North Dakota.</i>

1225
01:08:20.264 --> 01:08:23.559
<i>But he never accepted them
as his parents at all.</i>

1226
01:08:24.268 --> 01:08:28.188
<i>In his own imagination,
he was a son of God...</i>

1227
01:08:29.481 --> 01:08:30.733
<i>...destined for...</i>

1228
01:08:30.941 --> 01:08:32.443
<i>...future glory.</i>

1229
01:08:33.235 --> 01:08:38.574
<i>Chasing this destiny, a 16-year-old
Gatz ran far, far away.</i>

1230
01:08:39.033 --> 01:08:43.287
<i>One afternoon, off the coast of Lake
Superior, he spotted a yacht in peril.</i>

1231
01:08:44.079 --> 01:08:45.914
<i>He rowed out and rescued the vessel...</i>

1232
01:08:46.123 --> 01:08:49.918
<i>...and its captain,
alcoholic millionaire Dan Cody.</i>

1233
01:08:50.502 --> 01:08:51.462
Where's the boat?

1234
01:08:52.463 --> 01:08:53.903
You're gonna hit the shoal!

1235
01:08:53.922 --> 01:08:55.257
We're gonna hit the shoal!

1236
01:08:55.466 --> 01:08:56.666
What the hell are you doing...

1237
01:08:58.135 --> 01:09:00.554
- ...old sport?
<i>- This was...</i>

1238
01:09:00.763 --> 01:09:03.098
<i>...his opportunity and he seized it.</i>

1239
01:09:03.265 --> 01:09:04.265
<i>And I decided...</i>

1240
01:09:04.350 --> 01:09:07.436
...right then and there
to call myself Jay Gatsby.

1241
01:09:07.645 --> 01:09:09.085
<i>He sailed the yacht out of danger...</i>

1242
01:09:09.229 --> 01:09:11.523
<i>...and into his future.</i>

1243
01:09:12.524 --> 01:09:14.693
<i>Gatsby showed skill and ambition.</i>

1244
01:09:14.902 --> 01:09:17.529
<i>And for five years, they sailed the world.</i>

1245
01:09:17.738 --> 01:09:19.198
<i>He was all right, old Dan.</i>

1246
01:09:19.698 --> 01:09:20.991
He taught me everything.

1247
01:09:21.200 --> 01:09:23.202
<i>How to dress, act
and speak like a gentleman.</i>

1248
01:09:23.369 --> 01:09:25.329
Gatsby. Jay Gatsby.

1249
01:09:25.537 --> 01:09:27.206
She looks well, doesn't she...

1250
01:09:27.498 --> 01:09:29.375
<i>Gatsby hoped to inherit Cody's fortune.</i>

1251
01:09:29.583 --> 01:09:31.585
<i>- ...old sport?
- But when Cody died...</i>

1252
01:09:31.794 --> 01:09:35.506
<i>Gatsby was cheated of his inheritance
by Cody's family.</i>

1253
01:09:35.714 --> 01:09:38.926
<i>He'd been left with the ability
to play the gentleman...</i>

1254
01:09:39.134 --> 01:09:42.513
<i>...but he was once again dirt-poor.</i>

1255
01:09:44.973 --> 01:09:50.104
<i>By midsummer,
Gatsby was from' page news.</i>

1256
01:10:01.532 --> 01:10:03.992
<i>Where did the money come from?</i>

1257
01:10:17.297 --> 01:10:18.674
<i>That's what all of New York...</i>

1258
01:10:18.841 --> 01:10:20.426
<i>...wanted to know.</i>

1259
01:10:20.592 --> 01:10:23.846
<i>And it was the same question
on Tom's mind...</i>

1260
01:10:24.012 --> 01:10:29.351
<i>...when he accompanied Daisy
to one of Gatsby's glittering parties.</i>

1261
01:10:30.853 --> 01:10:32.187
I'll be right back.

1262
01:10:32.938 --> 01:10:34.019
You know, a lot of these...

1263
01:10:34.148 --> 01:10:35.691
...newly rich people are just...

1264
01:10:35.858 --> 01:10:37.860
- ...filthy bootleggers.
- Not Gatsby.

1265
01:10:38.026 --> 01:10:40.112
He's a businessman.
He owned a lot of drugstores.

1266
01:10:40.279 --> 01:10:41.155
Businessman!

1267
01:10:41.321 --> 01:10:43.907
May I introduce Senator Gulick?
This is...

1268
01:10:44.074 --> 01:10:45.325
- ...Mr. Carraway.
- Senator.

1269
01:10:46.577 --> 01:10:48.829
- Mrs. Buchanan.
- Charmed.

1270
01:10:48.996 --> 01:10:50.539
Delighted.

1271
01:10:50.706 --> 01:10:51.790
Oh, and Mr. Buchanan...

1272
01:10:51.957 --> 01:10:53.792
...the polo player.

1273
01:10:55.711 --> 01:10:57.379
- No, not me.
- Always a pleasure...

1274
01:10:57.546 --> 01:10:58.756
...to meet a Buchanan.

1275
01:10:58.922 --> 01:11:00.758
- Likewise.
- I'll catch up with you later.

1276
01:11:00.924 --> 01:11:04.011
- Perhaps at the craps table.
- I'd rather not be the polo player.

1277
01:11:04.178 --> 01:11:07.598
Tom. You should be proud
of your achievements.

1278
01:11:09.516 --> 01:11:11.351
May I show you around?

1279
01:11:11.518 --> 01:11:12.770
Sure.

1280
01:11:14.980 --> 01:11:16.020
You must know the faces...

1281
01:11:16.148 --> 01:11:18.228
- ...of many people you've heard of!
- Absolutely.

1282
01:11:18.233 --> 01:11:21.069
We don't go around very much.
I don't think I recognize...

1283
01:11:21.236 --> 01:11:23.197
- ...a single person.
- Is that so?

1284
01:11:23.363 --> 01:11:24.740
Perhaps you know that lady...

1285
01:11:24.907 --> 01:11:28.368
...right there. DAISY: It's Marlene Moon.
I adore her pictures.

1286
01:11:28.535 --> 01:11:30.662
Would you like me to introduce you?

1287
01:11:32.581 --> 01:11:35.125
I'd really rather not be the polo player.

1288
01:11:41.799 --> 01:11:43.675
These things excite me so.

1289
01:11:44.593 --> 01:11:45.593
Nice little dance.

1290
01:11:49.807 --> 01:11:51.934
I believe we've met before,
Mr. Buchanan.

1291
01:11:52.100 --> 01:11:53.811
About a month ago.

1292
01:11:56.438 --> 01:11:58.273
That's right.

1293
01:11:58.482 --> 01:11:59.983
And you were with Nick here.

1294
01:12:00.150 --> 01:12:02.110
- At the barbershop.
- No, thank you.

1295
01:12:02.736 --> 01:12:04.029
That's right.

1296
01:12:04.571 --> 01:12:06.365
See, I know your wife.

1297
01:12:08.867 --> 01:12:09.910
That so?

1298
01:12:10.536 --> 01:12:11.537
Yes.

1299
01:12:15.791 --> 01:12:17.334
Mr. Gatsby, sir.

1300
01:12:18.585 --> 01:12:20.879
- Mr. Slagle is here.
- Not now. Not now.

1301
01:12:23.215 --> 01:12:25.259
"The Foxtrot."

1302
01:12:39.273 --> 01:12:40.774
Mr. Buchanan.

1303
01:12:42.401 --> 01:12:44.069
Would you mind terribly?

1304
01:12:46.822 --> 01:12:48.073
Of course not.

1305
01:12:52.286 --> 01:12:53.912
I think I can keep myself amused.

1306
01:12:55.914 --> 01:12:58.750
In case you need to take down
any addresses.

1307
01:13:11.722 --> 01:13:14.224
Please move aside. Excuse me.

1308
01:13:18.020 --> 01:13:19.479
Another swell party, Jay.

1309
01:13:19.646 --> 01:13:22.274
Be careful for the snake charmer,
Mr. McLennehan.

1310
01:13:23.025 --> 01:13:24.465
Is all this made entirely...

1311
01:13:24.526 --> 01:13:26.737
...from your own imagination?

1312
01:13:27.738 --> 01:13:28.822
No.

1313
01:13:30.157 --> 01:13:32.451
You see, you were there all along...

1314
01:13:33.160 --> 01:13:35.078
...in every idea...

1315
01:13:35.787 --> 01:13:37.414
...in every decision.

1316
01:13:38.457 --> 01:13:40.626
Of course, if anything
is not to your liking...

1317
01:13:40.792 --> 01:13:42.169
...I'll change it.

1318
01:13:45.213 --> 01:13:46.673
It's perfect.

1319
01:13:47.758 --> 01:13:49.301
From your perfect...

1320
01:13:49.468 --> 01:13:51.595
...irresistible imagination.

1321
01:13:56.266 --> 01:13:57.017
I wonder...

1322
01:13:57.184 --> 01:13:59.186
...where the devil he met Daisy.

1323
01:14:07.778 --> 01:14:10.280
- I'll find you.
- All right.

1324
01:14:14.576 --> 01:14:15.953
Come with me.

1325
01:14:18.830 --> 01:14:19.790
Coming through, please.

1326
01:14:19.957 --> 01:14:21.124
Pardon me.

1327
01:14:24.252 --> 01:14:25.587
Thank you.

1328
01:15:45.292 --> 01:15:47.711
There's a man.
There's a man.

1329
01:15:48.378 --> 01:15:50.088
Have you seen my wife?

1330
01:15:52.716 --> 01:15:53.967
No.

1331
01:15:54.593 --> 01:15:55.927
Not for a while.

1332
01:15:59.389 --> 01:16:00.640
Whiskey.

1333
01:16:01.016 --> 01:16:03.602
It's funny, the senator
just saw her down here.

1334
01:16:07.981 --> 01:16:10.609
I wish we could just run away.

1335
01:16:20.077 --> 01:16:21.078
Run away?

1336
01:16:21.870 --> 01:16:22.871
No.

1337
01:16:23.497 --> 01:16:25.415
Daisy, darling, that...

1338
01:16:25.582 --> 01:16:28.168
- That wouldn't be respectable.
<i>- You live...</i>

1339
01:16:28.335 --> 01:16:29.211
...around here, Nick?

1340
01:16:32.005 --> 01:16:33.256
Just next door.

1341
01:16:34.341 --> 01:16:36.051
Is that so?

1342
01:16:39.471 --> 01:16:41.056
<i>We're gonna live here...</i>

1343
01:16:41.431 --> 01:16:42.724
...in this house.

1344
01:16:43.391 --> 01:16:44.059
You...

1345
01:16:44.643 --> 01:16:46.144
...and me.

1346
01:16:49.439 --> 01:16:50.607
Darling...

1347
01:16:53.026 --> 01:16:54.402
...it's time to tell Tom.

1348
01:16:57.614 --> 01:16:58.535
Come on, boys.

1349
01:16:58.698 --> 01:16:59.366
Scram!

1350
01:17:00.575 --> 01:17:01.701
Good night, gentlemen.

1351
01:17:03.495 --> 01:17:04.621
What a circus.

1352
01:17:08.667 --> 01:17:09.376
Well...

1353
01:17:09.543 --> 01:17:11.545
...if you see her...

1354
01:17:12.295 --> 01:17:14.256
...I'll be looking for her.

1355
01:17:14.422 --> 01:17:15.507
You tell her.

1356
01:17:16.800 --> 01:17:18.218
Remember how much fun we had?

1357
01:17:18.385 --> 01:17:20.887
I don't know why we can't
just have fun like that again.

1358
01:17:21.054 --> 01:17:22.055
Hello?

1359
01:17:25.267 --> 01:17:26.601
Hello, Nicky.

1360
01:17:26.768 --> 01:17:28.103
We're having a row.

1361
01:17:28.270 --> 01:17:30.105
- What about?
- About...

1362
01:17:30.272 --> 01:17:31.314
...things.

1363
01:17:32.732 --> 01:17:34.359
About the future.

1364
01:17:36.027 --> 01:17:36.945
The future of...

1365
01:17:37.112 --> 01:17:38.572
...the colored empires.

1366
01:17:40.031 --> 01:17:40.991
It's Tom.

1367
01:17:41.158 --> 01:17:43.618
- He's wandering around the
party looking... HERZOG: Sir.

1368
01:17:44.828 --> 01:17:45.787
Mr. Gatsby, sir.

1369
01:17:46.121 --> 01:17:49.583
It's Mr. Slagle. He's quite emotional.

1370
01:17:50.750 --> 01:17:52.210
Excuse me.

1371
01:17:56.006 --> 01:17:58.675
Nick. Would you mind terribly?

1372
01:17:58.842 --> 01:18:00.385
Of course.

1373
01:18:03.847 --> 01:18:05.182
<i>Gatsby disappeared...</i>

1374
01:18:05.348 --> 01:18:08.602
<i>...to deal with a dispute of some sort.</i>

1375
01:18:09.936 --> 01:18:11.980
<i>Daisy waited...</i>

1376
01:18:15.317 --> 01:18:18.445
<i>...but Gatsby was unable to return.</i>

1377
01:18:21.031 --> 01:18:24.242
With these hot-headed types
in here, I rely on you.

1378
01:18:24.409 --> 01:18:26.995
But you were not available.
A scene was made.

1379
01:18:27.954 --> 01:18:29.289
What's going on with you, Jay?

1380
01:18:32.626 --> 01:18:33.793
Where were you?

1381
01:18:33.960 --> 01:18:37.047
With Nicky. Mr. Gatsby
was showing us the grounds.

1382
01:18:37.881 --> 01:18:39.121
He certainly must have...

1383
01:18:39.132 --> 01:18:41.509
...strained himself
to get this menagerie together.

1384
01:18:44.137 --> 01:18:47.974
I'd like to know who he is
and what he does.

1385
01:18:48.767 --> 01:18:51.770
And I think I'll make a point
of finding out.

1386
01:19:09.120 --> 01:19:11.881
- I'm not sure. Ask them in the kitchen.
- To the left.

1387
01:19:12.832 --> 01:19:13.592
Yeah...

1388
01:19:13.667 --> 01:19:16.962
...we'll pile that stuff in the garage,
it's going back to town.

1389
01:19:30.100 --> 01:19:30.767
<i>Well...</i>

1390
01:19:30.934 --> 01:19:32.894
...there you are. Daisy just left.

1391
01:19:33.061 --> 01:19:34.521
She asked me to tell you...

1392
01:19:34.688 --> 01:19:35.814
...she had a wonderful time.

1393
01:19:36.398 --> 01:19:37.565
She didn't like it.

1394
01:19:37.732 --> 01:19:38.858
Of course she did.

1395
01:19:39.150 --> 01:19:40.610
No, no, no.

1396
01:19:41.486 --> 01:19:45.031
No, she didn't like it.
She did not have a good time.

1397
01:19:45.782 --> 01:19:46.491
I feel so far...

1398
01:19:46.658 --> 01:19:47.659
...away from her now.

1399
01:19:47.826 --> 01:19:49.703
It's so hard to make her understand.

1400
01:19:49.869 --> 01:19:51.162
You mean about the party?

1401
01:19:51.329 --> 01:19:52.330
The party?

1402
01:19:54.624 --> 01:19:57.043
I couldn't care less about the parties.

1403
01:19:57.210 --> 01:19:58.878
That'll be all for now, gentlemen.

1404
01:19:59.045 --> 01:20:00.565
- Thank you.
- Thank you, sir.

1405
01:20:01.172 --> 01:20:02.007
You see...

1406
01:20:04.342 --> 01:20:07.429
...she has to tell Tom
that she never loved him.

1407
01:20:07.595 --> 01:20:08.430
What?

1408
01:20:08.596 --> 01:20:09.764
Yes.

1409
01:20:09.931 --> 01:20:12.976
Then we can go back to Louisville
to her parents' house.

1410
01:20:13.601 --> 01:20:16.771
Her parents are lovely people,
old sport. We'll be...

1411
01:20:16.938 --> 01:20:17.939
...married there.

1412
01:20:19.149 --> 01:20:20.150
See...

1413
01:20:20.817 --> 01:20:24.404
See, Daisy and I are gonna start over
just as if it were five years ago.

1414
01:20:24.571 --> 01:20:26.156
I wouldn't ask too much of her.

1415
01:20:26.323 --> 01:20:27.615
Wouldn't ask too much?

1416
01:20:27.782 --> 01:20:28.825
No.

1417
01:20:33.121 --> 01:20:35.332
I beg your pardon, old sport. It's just...

1418
01:20:36.291 --> 01:20:38.418
It's so sad,
because it's so hard to make her...

1419
01:20:38.585 --> 01:20:40.253
...understand.

1420
01:20:40.587 --> 01:20:43.465
It's so hard to make her understand.

1421
01:20:43.673 --> 01:20:49.012
I've gotten all these things for her,
and now she just wants to run away.

1422
01:20:50.347 --> 01:20:51.139
She even wants...

1423
01:20:51.306 --> 01:20:52.849
- ...to leave that.
- Jay.

1424
01:20:54.059 --> 01:20:56.269
You can't repeat the past.

1425
01:20:57.645 --> 01:20:59.564
Can't repeat the past?

1426
01:20:59.773 --> 01:21:01.024
No.

1427
01:21:02.192 --> 01:21:04.194
Why, of course you can.

1428
01:21:04.903 --> 01:21:06.571
Of course you can.

1429
01:21:07.906 --> 01:21:09.032
I'm gonna fix things...

1430
01:21:09.199 --> 01:21:11.076
...just the way they were before.

1431
01:21:11.618 --> 01:21:16.247
Everything's been so confused
since then. I...

1432
01:21:17.374 --> 01:21:20.043
<i>He talked a lot about the past...</i>

1433
01:21:20.585 --> 01:21:22.629
<i>...as if he wanted to recover something.</i>

1434
01:21:23.254 --> 01:21:28.051
If I could just get back to the start...

1435
01:21:29.552 --> 01:21:31.721
If I could just get back to the start...

1436
01:21:31.888 --> 01:21:33.890
...I could find it again.

1437
01:21:34.182 --> 01:21:35.642
<i>Some vision...</i>

1438
01:21:35.809 --> 01:21:39.604
<i>...of himself that he had
put into loving Daisy.</i>

1439
01:21:40.772 --> 01:21:44.567
<i>One night in Louisville, five years ago...</i>

1440
01:21:44.734 --> 01:21:50.073
<i>Gatsby found himself at Daisy's
house by colossal accident.</i>

1441
01:21:50.448 --> 01:21:54.160
<i>I went to her house first with some
of the officers from Camp Taylor.</i>

1442
01:21:54.577 --> 01:21:56.538
<i>I'd never been in such a beautiful house.</i>

1443
01:21:56.704 --> 01:21:58.206
May I save the next dance?

1444
01:21:58.373 --> 01:21:59.916
<i>His uniform hid...</i>

1445
01:22:00.083 --> 01:22:01.626
<i>...the truth that he</i> was...

1446
01:22:01.793 --> 01:22:03.253
<i>...a penniless young man...</i>

1447
01:22:03.878 --> 01:22:04.921
<i>...with only that...</i>

1448
01:22:05.088 --> 01:22:07.257
<i>grand vision of himself.</i>

1449
01:22:07.424 --> 01:22:09.384
- Daisy, don't scamper.
- I was not, Mother.

1450
01:22:09.551 --> 01:22:11.678
So many dashing young officers here.

1451
01:22:11.845 --> 01:22:14.222
And from such illustrious families.

1452
01:22:14.389 --> 01:22:16.891
<i>I always knew that I could climb.</i>

1453
01:22:17.392 --> 01:22:18.393
<i>But I could only climb...</i>

1454
01:22:18.560 --> 01:22:20.937
<i>...if I climbed alone.</i>

1455
01:22:24.274 --> 01:22:24.941
<i>I knew...</i>

1456
01:22:25.108 --> 01:22:26.693
...that when I...

1457
01:22:27.026 --> 01:22:28.903
...kissed this girl...

1458
01:22:30.613 --> 01:22:31.364
...I would be...

1459
01:22:31.531 --> 01:22:33.533
...forever wed to her.

1460
01:22:37.704 --> 01:22:39.539
So I stopped.

1461
01:22:41.833 --> 01:22:43.460
I stopped and I...

1462
01:22:44.377 --> 01:22:45.837
I waited.

1463
01:22:47.297 --> 01:22:47.964
<i>I waited...</i>

1464
01:22:48.131 --> 01:22:49.883
<i>...for a moment longer.</i>

1465
01:22:50.049 --> 01:22:51.342
<i>He knew...</i>

1466
01:22:51.509 --> 01:22:56.473
...his mind would never again
be free to romp like...

1467
01:22:57.515 --> 01:22:59.642
<i>...the mind of God.</i>

1468
01:23:02.562 --> 01:23:04.189
<i>That falling in love...</i>

1469
01:23:04.355 --> 01:23:06.608
<i>...would change his destiny...</i>

1470
01:23:07.609 --> 01:23:08.985
<i>...forever.</i>

1471
01:23:13.072 --> 01:23:15.617
And then I just let myself go.

1472
01:23:24.209 --> 01:23:27.337
<i>She blossomed for him
like a flower...</i>

1473
01:23:29.881 --> 01:23:32.300
<i>...and the incarnation was complete.</i>

1474
01:23:42.227 --> 01:23:46.272
I knew it was a great mistake
for a man like me to fall in love.

1475
01:23:46.689 --> 01:23:50.235
I'm only 32. I might still be a great man
if I could forget that I once...

1476
01:23:50.401 --> 01:23:51.861
...lost Daisy, but...

1477
01:23:52.820 --> 01:23:55.406
...my life, old sport, my life...

1478
01:23:55.865 --> 01:23:57.909
My life has got to be like this.

1479
01:24:02.247 --> 01:24:03.581
It's got to keep going up.

1480
01:24:10.588 --> 01:24:12.840
She has to go to Tom...

1481
01:24:13.550 --> 01:24:16.427
...and tell him that she never loved him.

1482
01:24:21.474 --> 01:24:24.227
I just need to give her
more time, old sport.

1483
01:24:24.394 --> 01:24:25.937
More time.

1484
01:24:32.193 --> 01:24:34.988
Don't worry, old sport. Don't worry.

1485
01:24:35.154 --> 01:24:37.073
I can protect her here.

1486
01:24:39.367 --> 01:24:41.035
Good night, old sport.

1487
01:24:49.127 --> 01:24:50.878
You're wrong about the past, old sport.

1488
01:24:54.173 --> 01:24:55.675
You're wrong.

1489
01:25:20.116 --> 01:25:21.996
<i>There had been music
from my neighbor's...</i>

1490
01:25:22.160 --> 01:25:23.161
...all summer.

1491
01:25:23.328 --> 01:25:26.164
In his blue gardens, men and girls
came and went like moths...

1492
01:25:26.331 --> 01:25:30.001
...among the whisperings
and the laughter and the stars.

1493
01:25:30.501 --> 01:25:31.753
Breakfast.

1494
01:25:31.919 --> 01:25:32.920
Yes.

1495
01:25:33.087 --> 01:25:34.672
Thank you, thank you.

1496
01:25:35.548 --> 01:25:36.924
Laughter.

1497
01:25:37.842 --> 01:25:39.093
Thank you.

1498
01:25:40.011 --> 01:25:43.556
No. The whisperings
and the champagne and the stars.

1499
01:25:45.099 --> 01:25:46.142
But...

1500
01:25:47.477 --> 01:25:50.271
...after Tom and Daisy's visit...

1501
01:25:51.105 --> 01:25:54.150
<i>...Gatsby's lights went out one by one.</i>

1502
01:25:54.901 --> 01:25:56.944
<i>There were no more parties.</i>

1503
01:25:59.405 --> 01:26:01.616
<i>Daisy visited discreetly.</i>

1504
01:26:05.286 --> 01:26:08.039
<i>For the very same fame
that had all summer...</i>

1505
01:26:08.206 --> 01:26:11.751
<i>...been a source of satisfaction
to Jay Gatsby...</i>

1506
01:26:12.502 --> 01:26:14.253
<i>...had become a threat.</i>

1507
01:26:43.658 --> 01:26:45.827
I don't wanna go home.

1508
01:26:48.705 --> 01:26:51.708
<i>I hear you fired all your servants.</i>

1509
01:26:51.874 --> 01:26:54.961
Daisy comes over sometimes
in the afternoon.

1510
01:26:55.128 --> 01:27:00.341
I wanted people who wouldn't gossip
until we decide what we're going to do.

1511
01:27:00.967 --> 01:27:01.968
You see, these towns...

1512
01:27:02.135 --> 01:27:05.304
<i>...are very close together, old sport...</i>

1513
01:27:05.471 --> 01:27:07.932
...and, well, it gets in the papers,
you understand.

1514
01:27:08.099 --> 01:27:10.893
They're all people Wolfshiem
wanted to do something for.

1515
01:27:11.436 --> 01:27:13.646
<i>What's the difference
as long as they can cook...</i>

1516
01:27:13.813 --> 01:27:14.939
...and make beds, right?

1517
01:27:19.235 --> 01:27:20.069
Nick...

1518
01:27:20.236 --> 01:27:22.780
<i>...Daisy's ready. She's ready.</i>

1519
01:27:22.947 --> 01:27:24.699
There's just one thing.

1520
01:27:24.866 --> 01:27:30.204
She's requested that you and Miss Baker
be there for lunch tomorrow at her house.

1521
01:27:31.664 --> 01:27:33.458
Will you come, old sport?

1522
01:27:34.459 --> 01:27:36.043
<i>Daisy needs you.</i>

1523
01:27:37.962 --> 01:27:39.422
I...

1524
01:27:39.922 --> 01:27:41.507
We need you.

1525
01:27:48.473 --> 01:27:50.266
Can you come, old sport?

1526
01:28:07.658 --> 01:28:09.285
You know, I read somewhere...

1527
01:28:09.494 --> 01:28:12.288
...that the sun
is getting hotter every year.

1528
01:28:13.581 --> 01:28:14.540
Wait a minute.

1529
01:28:15.124 --> 01:28:16.375
It's the opposite.

1530
01:28:16.751 --> 01:28:17.877
Sun is getting colder...

1531
01:28:18.044 --> 01:28:19.295
...every year.

1532
01:28:21.297 --> 01:28:23.424
Oh, I would like to be out...

1533
01:28:23.591 --> 01:28:25.176
...on that bay today.

1534
01:28:28.429 --> 01:28:30.223
I'm right across from you.

1535
01:28:33.643 --> 01:28:34.894
Right there.

1536
01:28:38.523 --> 01:28:39.941
Oh.

1537
01:28:40.525 --> 01:28:42.109
So you are.

1538
01:28:47.532 --> 01:28:48.574
You see...

1539
01:28:49.033 --> 01:28:49.742
...every night...

1540
01:28:49.909 --> 01:28:51.077
...I can see...

1541
01:28:51.786 --> 01:28:53.329
...that light...

1542
01:28:53.955 --> 01:28:55.706
...at the end of your dock blinking.

1543
01:28:57.166 --> 01:28:58.417
What light?

1544
01:29:06.759 --> 01:29:08.080
You see, Mr. Buchanan...

1545
01:29:08.094 --> 01:29:09.054
...I wanted to be close.

1546
01:29:15.685 --> 01:29:17.286
- Daisy and I have both...
- It's so hot!

1547
01:29:17.395 --> 01:29:18.104
Everything's...

1548
01:29:18.271 --> 01:29:19.355
...so confused.

1549
01:29:19.522 --> 01:29:21.023
What'll we do this afternoon...

1550
01:29:21.899 --> 01:29:23.899
...or the day after that,
or for the next 30 years?

1551
01:29:23.943 --> 01:29:24.744
Oh, don't be morbid.

1552
01:29:25.653 --> 01:29:27.363
Let's go to town.
Who wants to go to town?

1553
01:29:27.530 --> 01:29:28.197
Town?

1554
01:29:30.074 --> 01:29:31.367
Women get these...

1555
01:29:31.534 --> 01:29:32.285
...notions.

1556
01:29:51.512 --> 01:29:52.722
Daisy.

1557
01:29:58.394 --> 01:29:59.854
You look so cool.

1558
01:30:02.899 --> 01:30:05.359
You always look so cool...

1559
01:30:05.526 --> 01:30:06.652
...like the advertisement...

1560
01:30:06.819 --> 01:30:08.279
...of the man in Times Square.

1561
01:30:10.489 --> 01:30:11.157
The man...

1562
01:30:11.324 --> 01:30:13.492
...in the cool, beautiful shirts.

1563
01:30:19.916 --> 01:30:20.958
<i>She had told...</i>

1564
01:30:21.125 --> 01:30:22.418
<i>Gatsby that she loved him...</i>

1565
01:30:24.045 --> 01:30:24.712
<i>...and Tom...</i>

1566
01:30:24.879 --> 01:30:26.213
<i>...had seen.</i>

1567
01:30:27.340 --> 01:30:28.261
Let's go to town.

1568
01:30:28.883 --> 01:30:30.134
I'm perfectly willing.

1569
01:30:30.301 --> 01:30:31.385
It's a marvelous idea.

1570
01:30:31.552 --> 01:30:32.432
Henri! Have the car...

1571
01:30:32.553 --> 01:30:33.554
...brought around now.

1572
01:30:33.721 --> 01:30:34.889
Can't anyone at least have...

1573
01:30:35.056 --> 01:30:36.976
- ...a cigarette first?
- We smoked through lunch.

1574
01:30:37.016 --> 01:30:39.894
Come on, let's have fun.
It's too hot to fuss.

1575
01:30:40.061 --> 01:30:41.604
Daisy, it was your great idea.

1576
01:30:41.771 --> 01:30:43.314
Why don't we? Let's all go to town.

1577
01:30:43.564 --> 01:30:45.900
I changed my mind, you brute.

1578
01:30:46.067 --> 01:30:47.107
Come on. We'll get...

1579
01:30:47.109 --> 01:30:49.195
...a great big room at the Plaza...

1580
01:30:49.362 --> 01:30:50.488
...a bucket of ice...

1581
01:30:50.780 --> 01:30:51.948
...a bottle of whiskey...

1582
01:30:52.114 --> 01:30:54.158
...and it will be fun.

1583
01:30:54.325 --> 01:30:55.326
Come on.

1584
01:30:55.701 --> 01:30:56.994
It was your idea.

1585
01:30:58.871 --> 01:31:00.790
Fine. Have it your own way, Tom.

1586
01:31:00.957 --> 01:31:02.208
Come on, Jordan.

1587
01:31:07.421 --> 01:31:09.632
Will you join us, Mr. Gatsby?

1588
01:31:10.716 --> 01:31:12.476
Two bottles of whiskey.
Wrap them in a towel.

1589
01:31:12.802 --> 01:31:13.761
Come on, Nick!

1590
01:31:18.849 --> 01:31:19.934
Mr. Gatsby...

1591
01:31:20.851 --> 01:31:22.436
...would you be good enough to take...

1592
01:31:22.603 --> 01:31:24.438
...my coupe,
and I'll drive everyone else...

1593
01:31:24.605 --> 01:31:25.856
- ...in your circus wagon.
- I don't think...

1594
01:31:26.023 --> 01:31:27.775
...there's much gas, old sport.

1595
01:31:28.025 --> 01:31:29.235
No, plenty of gas.

1596
01:31:30.027 --> 01:31:31.320
Well, if I run out, I'll stop...

1597
01:31:31.487 --> 01:31:33.030
...at a drugstore. I hear you can...

1598
01:31:33.197 --> 01:31:34.758
...buy anything at a drugstore
nowadays.

1599
01:31:36.575 --> 01:31:37.576
You take Nick and Jordan.

1600
01:31:37.743 --> 01:31:38.903
I suppose you can...

1601
01:31:39.036 --> 01:31:42.081
- ...yes.
- We'll meet you at the Plaza.

1602
01:31:42.248 --> 01:31:43.165
I'll be the man on the corner...

1603
01:31:43.332 --> 01:31:45.084
...smoking two cigarettes!

1604
01:31:52.008 --> 01:31:54.093
You must think I'm pretty dumb,
don't you?

1605
01:31:54.260 --> 01:31:57.013
Well, I have a second sight sometimes
that tells me what to do.

1606
01:31:57.346 --> 01:31:59.266
I've made a small investigation
into this fellow.

1607
01:31:59.432 --> 01:32:00.632
And you found he was...

1608
01:32:00.641 --> 01:32:02.351
- ...an Oxford man?
- Oxford, New Mexico.

1609
01:32:02.518 --> 01:32:04.478
He wears a pink suit, for chrissake.

1610
01:32:29.003 --> 01:32:31.464
Tom, we're almost out of gas.

1611
01:32:38.304 --> 01:32:39.180
Wilson!

1612
01:32:39.805 --> 01:32:40.473
Wilson!

1613
01:32:40.765 --> 01:32:41.766
What are you waiting for?

1614
01:32:41.932 --> 01:32:43.768
- Let's have some gas!
- Uhh.

1615
01:32:44.310 --> 01:32:45.561
Do I have to do it myself?

1616
01:32:46.020 --> 01:32:47.621
You think I come here
to admire the view?

1617
01:32:47.772 --> 01:32:50.191
- I'm sorry, I'm sick.
- Why?

1618
01:32:51.358 --> 01:32:53.199
- Why, what's the matter?
- I don't know.

1619
01:32:53.652 --> 01:32:54.333
I'm all run down.

1620
01:32:55.321 --> 01:32:56.030
I need money...

1621
01:32:56.197 --> 01:32:57.615
- ...real bad.
- What?

1622
01:32:57.782 --> 01:32:59.492
My wife and I, we wanna go West.

1623
01:33:01.744 --> 01:33:02.745
Oh, your wife does?

1624
01:33:04.371 --> 01:33:05.498
<i>Tom was feeling...</i>

1625
01:33:05.664 --> 01:33:08.709
<i>...the hot whips of panic. His mistress...</i>

1626
01:33:08.876 --> 01:33:09.919
<i>...and wife...</i>

1627
01:33:10.086 --> 01:33:11.712
<i>...an hour ago so secure...</i>

1628
01:33:12.505 --> 01:33:14.757
<i>...were both slipping from his control.</i>

1629
01:33:15.674 --> 01:33:16.801
I wised up to something...

1630
01:33:16.967 --> 01:33:18.344
...funny these last two days.

1631
01:33:19.845 --> 01:33:20.846
Yeah, she's going West...

1632
01:33:21.013 --> 01:33:22.723
...whether she wants to or not.

1633
01:33:24.809 --> 01:33:25.601
What do I owe you?

1634
01:33:25.768 --> 01:33:26.685
A dollar.

1635
01:33:26.852 --> 01:33:28.938
- $1.20.
- You can have the car.

1636
01:33:29.105 --> 01:33:30.272
I'll send it over tomorrow!

1637
01:34:25.035 --> 01:34:26.787
Okay, fine, fine, fine.

1638
01:34:28.706 --> 01:34:30.332
Open another window.

1639
01:34:30.499 --> 01:34:31.542
There aren't any more.

1640
01:34:31.709 --> 01:34:32.709
Then telephone...

1641
01:34:32.793 --> 01:34:34.170
...for an ax.

1642
01:34:34.336 --> 01:34:36.005
Will you forget about the heat?

1643
01:34:36.422 --> 01:34:38.090
You make it worse by crabbing about it.

1644
01:34:38.257 --> 01:34:39.717
Why not let her alone, old sport?

1645
01:34:42.761 --> 01:34:44.180
That's a great expression...

1646
01:34:44.346 --> 01:34:45.681
...of yours, isn't it?

1647
01:34:45.848 --> 01:34:46.974
What is?

1648
01:34:47.516 --> 01:34:49.143
"Old sport."

1649
01:34:49.310 --> 01:34:50.811
- Where'd you pick it up?
- See here.

1650
01:34:50.978 --> 01:34:52.521
If you're going to make personal remarks...

1651
01:34:52.688 --> 01:34:54.398
...I won't stay here a minute.

1652
01:34:54.565 --> 01:34:56.233
Mr. Gatsby...

1653
01:34:56.734 --> 01:34:58.414
...I understand that you're an Oxford man.

1654
01:35:03.032 --> 01:35:04.283
No, not exactly, no.

1655
01:35:04.450 --> 01:35:05.743
Oh, yes, I understand that...

1656
01:35:05.910 --> 01:35:07.077
...you went to Oxford.

1657
01:35:09.288 --> 01:35:11.332
- Well, yes, I went there.
- Sure.

1658
01:35:12.166 --> 01:35:14.710
- The man in the pink suit went to Oxford.
- Tom.

1659
01:35:15.836 --> 01:35:17.463
I said I went there, didn't I?

1660
01:35:17.630 --> 01:35:18.797
Oh, I heard you.

1661
01:35:19.089 --> 01:35:21.050
I'd like to know when.

1662
01:35:21.675 --> 01:35:23.427
You'd like to know when.

1663
01:35:33.896 --> 01:35:35.189
Well, Mr. Gatsby?

1664
01:35:38.150 --> 01:35:40.736
It was in 1919.
I only stayed there five months.

1665
01:35:40.903 --> 01:35:43.030
That's why I can't exactly call myself...

1666
01:35:43.197 --> 01:35:44.615
...an Oxford man.

1667
01:35:44.782 --> 01:35:47.618
You see, it was an opportunity
they gave to some of us officers...

1668
01:35:47.785 --> 01:35:49.203
...who fought in the war.

1669
01:35:51.372 --> 01:35:52.581
<i>I wanted to get up and slap...</i>

1670
01:35:52.748 --> 01:35:54.124
<i>Gatsby on the back.</i>

1671
01:35:54.291 --> 01:35:55.691
I'll make you a drink, Tom.

1672
01:35:56.502 --> 01:35:59.022
- Then you won't seem so stupid to yourself.
- Wait a minute.

1673
01:35:59.713 --> 01:36:02.424
I want to ask Mr. Gatsby
one more question.

1674
01:36:02.591 --> 01:36:05.261
Oh, please, please, go on,
Mr. Buchanan. Go on.

1675
01:36:05.761 --> 01:36:09.640
What kind of a row are you trying
to cause in my house anyhow?

1676
01:36:10.182 --> 01:36:14.186
He isn't causing a row, you're causing
a row. Please have a little self-control.

1677
01:36:14.353 --> 01:36:15.771
Self-control?

1678
01:36:16.689 --> 01:36:17.481
Oh, I suppose...

1679
01:36:17.648 --> 01:36:19.692
...the latest thing is to sit back...

1680
01:36:19.858 --> 01:36:22.236
...and let Mr. Nobody From Nowhere...

1681
01:36:22.403 --> 01:36:23.904
...make love to your wife.

1682
01:36:24.071 --> 01:36:25.614
Well, if that's the idea...

1683
01:36:25.781 --> 01:36:26.949
...you can count me out.

1684
01:36:27.116 --> 01:36:28.075
See, nowadays people...

1685
01:36:28.242 --> 01:36:29.163
...begin by sneering...

1686
01:36:29.243 --> 01:36:31.370
...at family life and
family institutions...

1687
01:36:32.037 --> 01:36:33.517
...and the next you'll know, we'll...

1688
01:36:33.664 --> 01:36:35.416
...throw everything overboard,
we'll have intermarriage...

1689
01:36:35.582 --> 01:36:36.959
...between black and white!

1690
01:36:37.126 --> 01:36:38.566
Your wife doesn't love you.

1691
01:36:44.258 --> 01:36:44.925
She never...

1692
01:36:45.092 --> 01:36:46.051
...loved you.

1693
01:36:46.218 --> 01:36:47.553
You see, she loves me.

1694
01:36:52.433 --> 01:36:53.434
You must be crazy.

1695
01:36:53.892 --> 01:36:55.728
No, old sport.

1696
01:36:55.978 --> 01:36:59.273
No, you see, she never loved you.

1697
01:36:59.648 --> 01:37:01.358
She only married you
because I was poor...

1698
01:37:01.525 --> 01:37:03.605
...and she was tired of waiting.
It was a terrible...

1699
01:37:04.278 --> 01:37:07.072
...terrible mistake, but in her heart...

1700
01:37:07.239 --> 01:37:08.657
In her heart, she never loved...

1701
01:37:08.824 --> 01:37:10.117
...anyone but me.

1702
01:37:10.284 --> 01:37:11.785
- We should go.
- Let's all go home.

1703
01:37:11.952 --> 01:37:13.829
- Sit down, Daisy!
- Please.

1704
01:37:14.663 --> 01:37:15.464
Please, take a seat.

1705
01:37:16.707 --> 01:37:18.125
Go on, Daisy.

1706
01:37:19.835 --> 01:37:21.128
Daisy...

1707
01:37:22.504 --> 01:37:23.589
...what's been going on?

1708
01:37:23.756 --> 01:37:25.957
- I want to hear about it.
- I told you what's going on.

1709
01:37:26.050 --> 01:37:27.760
It's been going on for five years.

1710
01:37:28.052 --> 01:37:29.803
You've been seeing him...

1711
01:37:31.055 --> 01:37:32.348
...for five years?

1712
01:37:32.514 --> 01:37:35.809
No, no, no, not seeing.
Not seeing, we couldn't. But...

1713
01:37:35.976 --> 01:37:38.979
...both of us loved each other
all that time. Didn't we?

1714
01:37:39.355 --> 01:37:40.356
Oh, that's all.

1715
01:37:41.398 --> 01:37:43.108
Ha-ha-ha! You're crazy!

1716
01:37:43.734 --> 01:37:44.614
I can't speak about...

1717
01:37:44.735 --> 01:37:48.113
...what happened five years ago
because I didn't know Daisy then.

1718
01:37:48.280 --> 01:37:51.241
And I'll be damned if I see how
you got within a mile of her...

1719
01:37:51.408 --> 01:37:54.161
...unless you brought the groceries
to the back door.

1720
01:37:54.370 --> 01:37:55.579
But all the rest of that...

1721
01:37:55.746 --> 01:37:57.122
...is a goddamn lie.

1722
01:37:57.289 --> 01:37:57.998
Daisy loved me...

1723
01:37:58.165 --> 01:37:59.792
...when she married me...

1724
01:38:00.209 --> 01:38:01.627
- ...and she loves me now.
- No.

1725
01:38:01.794 --> 01:38:04.314
- No. I'm sorry, Mr. Buchanan, no.
- She does! She does, though.

1726
01:38:04.380 --> 01:38:05.255
No, she does, though.

1727
01:38:05.422 --> 01:38:08.008
She does. And what's
more, I love Daisy too.

1728
01:38:09.301 --> 01:38:10.260
No.

1729
01:38:11.387 --> 01:38:12.388
I love you, Daisy.

1730
01:38:13.472 --> 01:38:14.556
Now, once in a while...

1731
01:38:14.723 --> 01:38:17.184
...I go off on a spree. I always come back.

1732
01:38:17.351 --> 01:38:19.853
- A spree. TOM: And in my heart
I love her all the time.

1733
01:38:20.020 --> 01:38:21.355
You're revolting.

1734
01:38:21.522 --> 01:38:22.443
You know why we left...

1735
01:38:22.564 --> 01:38:24.566
...Chicago? I'm surprised
they didn't treat you...

1736
01:38:24.733 --> 01:38:26.276
...to the story of that little spree!

1737
01:38:26.443 --> 01:38:29.446
That's all over now, Daisy, darling.
That's all over.

1738
01:38:29.613 --> 01:38:31.156
Just tell him the truth. Go on.

1739
01:38:31.323 --> 01:38:35.202
That you never loved him
and this will all be wiped out forever.

1740
01:38:35.369 --> 01:38:36.495
How could I love him...

1741
01:38:36.662 --> 01:38:38.831
- ...possibly?
- Remember our plans.

1742
01:38:38.997 --> 01:38:40.877
You tell him that you never loved him
and all...

1743
01:38:41.041 --> 01:38:42.668
...this pain will be wiped out...

1744
01:38:42.835 --> 01:38:43.794
...forever.

1745
01:38:44.670 --> 01:38:45.796
Daisy.

1746
01:38:47.423 --> 01:38:49.466
Daisy, tell him.

1747
01:38:49.675 --> 01:38:51.009
I never loved him.

1748
01:38:52.719 --> 01:38:53.846
Never?

1749
01:38:54.179 --> 01:38:56.056
- No.
- No.

1750
01:38:57.349 --> 01:38:59.059
Not at Kapiolani?

1751
01:39:02.896 --> 01:39:06.108
Not that day I carried you down from
the Punch Bowl to keep your shoes dry?

1752
01:39:11.071 --> 01:39:11.905
Never?

1753
01:39:12.865 --> 01:39:14.616
Please don't.

1754
01:39:15.242 --> 01:39:16.410
Daisy.

1755
01:39:17.703 --> 01:39:19.413
There, Jay.

1756
01:39:20.038 --> 01:39:21.248
You want too much.

1757
01:39:21.707 --> 01:39:22.708
I love you now...

1758
01:39:22.875 --> 01:39:24.355
...isn't that enough? I can't help...

1759
01:39:24.501 --> 01:39:25.669
...what's past.

1760
01:39:25.836 --> 01:39:28.255
I did love him once,
but I loved you too.

1761
01:39:31.049 --> 01:39:33.427
You loved me too?

1762
01:39:33.594 --> 01:39:34.803
You loved me...?

1763
01:39:34.970 --> 01:39:35.971
Even that is a lie!

1764
01:39:36.138 --> 01:39:37.097
She didn't know you were alive!

1765
01:39:37.264 --> 01:39:38.932
There are things between Daisy and me...

1766
01:39:39.099 --> 01:39:40.460
...Gatsby, that you'll never know.

1767
01:39:40.601 --> 01:39:42.352
- Things that neither of us can ever forget.
- I need to...

1768
01:39:42.519 --> 01:39:43.687
...speak to Daisy alone.

1769
01:39:43.854 --> 01:39:47.191
You see, you've got her
all excited now, don't you...

1770
01:39:47.357 --> 01:39:49.193
- ...old sport? Daisy.
- Even alone...

1771
01:39:49.359 --> 01:39:52.154
...I can't say I never loved Torn.
It wouldn't be true.

1772
01:39:52.321 --> 01:39:53.561
- What?
- Of course it wouldn't.

1773
01:39:53.655 --> 01:39:55.775
- As if it mattered to you.
- Of course it matters!

1774
01:39:55.866 --> 01:39:57.667
I'm gonna take better care of you
from now on.

1775
01:39:57.826 --> 01:40:01.079
You're not taking care of Daisy
anymore. She's leaving you.

1776
01:40:01.246 --> 01:40:02.414
Nonsense!

1777
01:40:02.581 --> 01:40:04.458
- I am, though!
- No, no, no.

1778
01:40:04.625 --> 01:40:05.667
She is not leaving me.

1779
01:40:05.834 --> 01:40:06.752
Certainly not...

1780
01:40:06.919 --> 01:40:08.337
...for a common swindler...

1781
01:40:08.504 --> 01:40:09.505
...like you.

1782
01:40:09.671 --> 01:40:12.132
Mr. Gatsby, exactly who are you, anyhow?

1783
01:40:12.299 --> 01:40:13.342
I made an investigation...

1784
01:40:13.509 --> 01:40:14.909
...into your affairs. You're one...

1785
01:40:15.052 --> 01:40:16.252
...of Meyer Wolfshiem's bunch.

1786
01:40:16.386 --> 01:40:17.763
Please, let's go home.

1787
01:40:17.930 --> 01:40:19.348
See, he and this Wolfshiem...

1788
01:40:19.515 --> 01:40:20.933
...they bought up drugstores.

1789
01:40:21.099 --> 01:40:22.351
And sold bootlegged...

1790
01:40:22.518 --> 01:40:23.644
...alcohol over the counter.

1791
01:40:23.810 --> 01:40:25.145
What about it, old sport?

1792
01:40:25.312 --> 01:40:26.522
Don't you call me "old sport."

1793
01:40:26.688 --> 01:40:28.106
And this drugstore business...

1794
01:40:28.273 --> 01:40:30.776
...is just small change
compared to this bonds stunt...

1795
01:40:30.943 --> 01:40:32.544
...that you and Wolfshiem
have got going.

1796
01:40:32.569 --> 01:40:33.650
Your friend Walter Chase...

1797
01:40:33.779 --> 01:40:35.419
- ...isn't too proud to come in?
- I gave.

1798
01:40:35.572 --> 01:40:36.412
...that some thought.

1799
01:40:36.573 --> 01:40:37.908
How does a reputable banker...

1800
01:40:38.075 --> 01:40:39.743
...like Walter Chase find himself...

1801
01:40:39.910 --> 01:40:41.370
...up to his eyeballs in debt...

1802
01:40:41.537 --> 01:40:43.372
- I'll tell you.
- ...to a little kike like Wolfshiem?

1803
01:40:43.539 --> 01:40:45.791
- It's called greed, old sport.
- That's right!

1804
01:40:46.124 --> 01:40:48.919
And you have half of Wall Street
out there swilling your...

1805
01:40:49.086 --> 01:40:51.838
...free booze at that fun park
every weekend.

1806
01:40:52.005 --> 01:40:53.765
I'm surprised he hasn't
tried to drag you in.

1807
01:40:56.301 --> 01:40:57.427
My God, he has.

1808
01:40:57.594 --> 01:40:58.845
He's got nothing to do with...

1809
01:40:59.012 --> 01:41:00.305
With your little racket.

1810
01:41:02.641 --> 01:41:03.850
Daisy.

1811
01:41:04.059 --> 01:41:05.310
Daisy.

1812
01:41:06.270 --> 01:41:08.564
Can't you see who this guy is...

1813
01:41:08.730 --> 01:41:13.610
...with his house and his parties
and his fancy clothes?

1814
01:41:14.278 --> 01:41:16.238
He is just a front...

1815
01:41:16.405 --> 01:41:18.532
...for Wolfshiem, a gangster...

1816
01:41:18.699 --> 01:41:21.059
...to get his claws into respectable folk
like Walter Chase.

1817
01:41:21.201 --> 01:41:24.496
The only respectable thing about you,
old sport, is your money.

1818
01:41:24.663 --> 01:41:26.504
Your money, that's it.
Now I've just as much...

1819
01:41:26.665 --> 01:41:28.041
...as you. That means we're equal.

1820
01:41:28.208 --> 01:41:29.167
Oh, no. No.

1821
01:41:30.586 --> 01:41:31.587
We're different.

1822
01:41:32.588 --> 01:41:33.964
I am.

1823
01:41:34.256 --> 01:41:35.465
They are.

1824
01:41:35.632 --> 01:41:36.758
She is.

1825
01:41:36.925 --> 01:41:38.260
We're all different from you.

1826
01:41:38.844 --> 01:41:40.387
You see, we were born...

1827
01:41:40.846 --> 01:41:42.389
...different.

1828
01:41:43.348 --> 01:41:44.850
It's in our blood...

1829
01:41:45.225 --> 01:41:48.979
...and nothing that you do
or say or steal...

1830
01:41:49.396 --> 01:41:51.732
...or dream up can ever change that.

1831
01:41:52.608 --> 01:41:53.859
A girl like Daisy...

1832
01:41:54.026 --> 01:41:55.235
You shut up!

1833
01:41:55.402 --> 01:41:56.153
Shut up!

1834
01:41:56.320 --> 01:41:58.655
You shut up! Shut up!

1835
01:41:58.822 --> 01:42:00.532
Shut up!

1836
01:42:08.498 --> 01:42:11.126
<i>Gatsby looked, in that moment...</i>

1837
01:42:11.835 --> 01:42:13.587
<i>...as if he had...</i>

1838
01:42:14.212 --> 01:42:15.964
<i>...killed a man.</i>

1839
01:42:31.229 --> 01:42:33.065
My sincerest...

1840
01:42:33.482 --> 01:42:35.150
My sincerest apologies.

1841
01:42:39.446 --> 01:42:40.989
I seem to have...

1842
01:42:41.990 --> 01:42:43.659
...lost my temper.

1843
01:42:43.950 --> 01:42:45.494
That's right, Mr. Gatsby.

1844
01:42:47.162 --> 01:42:48.664
Show us some of those fine...

1845
01:42:48.830 --> 01:42:50.631
- ...Oxford manners.
- Daisy, darling.

1846
01:42:51.500 --> 01:42:54.753
None of this has any consequence.

1847
01:42:55.671 --> 01:42:56.755
Daisy.

1848
01:42:57.673 --> 01:42:59.841
Daisy, talk to me, darling.

1849
01:43:00.092 --> 01:43:02.386
I just lost my temper, that's all.

1850
01:43:02.552 --> 01:43:03.720
<i>He began talking...</i>

1851
01:43:03.887 --> 01:43:06.306
<i>...excitedly, but with every word...</i>

1852
01:43:06.473 --> 01:43:09.226
<i>...Daisy was drawing
further and further into herself.</i>

1853
01:43:09.393 --> 01:43:11.269
...to go to your parents' house
in Louisville.

1854
01:43:11.436 --> 01:43:12.357
Please, Tom, I can't...

1855
01:43:12.479 --> 01:43:14.064
...stand it anymore!

1856
01:43:17.109 --> 01:43:19.611
Why don't you two start on home...

1857
01:43:20.487 --> 01:43:21.571
...in Mr. Gatsby's car.

1858
01:43:21.863 --> 01:43:23.490
- Daisy, darling, look at me.
- Go on.

1859
01:43:23.657 --> 01:43:24.658
Daisy.

1860
01:43:24.825 --> 01:43:25.826
He won't annoy you.

1861
01:43:26.576 --> 01:43:27.456
I think he realizes...

1862
01:43:27.577 --> 01:43:30.080
- ...that this little flirtation is over.
- Daisy?!

1863
01:43:30.580 --> 01:43:31.623
Daisy!

1864
01:43:45.637 --> 01:43:47.431
You want any of this?

1865
01:43:47.848 --> 01:43:48.932
Jordan?

1866
01:43:49.099 --> 01:43:50.142
Nick?

1867
01:43:52.978 --> 01:43:54.020
Nick.

1868
01:43:54.646 --> 01:43:55.313
What?

1869
01:43:56.732 --> 01:43:58.066
You want any?

1870
01:43:58.442 --> 01:43:59.651
No.

1871
01:44:00.736 --> 01:44:02.362
I just remembered...

1872
01:44:04.614 --> 01:44:05.866
...today's my birthday.

1873
01:44:10.537 --> 01:44:11.788
Happy birthday.

1874
01:44:14.708 --> 01:44:18.962
<i>Thirty. The promise of
a decade of loneliness.</i>

1875
01:44:19.129 --> 01:44:22.591
<i>The formidable stroke of 30
died away...</i>

1876
01:44:22.758 --> 01:44:27.220
<i>...as Gatsby and Daisy drove on
through the cooling twilight...</i>

1877
01:44:29.097 --> 01:44:31.016
<i>...towards death.</i>

1878
01:44:34.603 --> 01:44:35.771
Don't you lie to me!

1879
01:44:36.438 --> 01:44:37.773
Hey, where'd you get these from?

1880
01:44:37.939 --> 01:44:39.483
You might fool me, but you don't...

1881
01:44:39.649 --> 01:44:40.442
...fool God!

1882
01:44:40.609 --> 01:44:42.152
God sees everything!

1883
01:44:44.780 --> 01:44:46.448
Myrtle!

1884
01:44:46.615 --> 01:44:47.824
Where are you?

1885
01:44:54.039 --> 01:44:55.207
Tom?

1886
01:44:55.373 --> 01:44:56.500
Torn!

1887
01:44:57.334 --> 01:44:58.335
Torn!

1888
01:44:59.002 --> 01:44:59.878
Myrtle!

1889
01:45:00.045 --> 01:45:01.005
I'm here! Stop!

1890
01:45:01.046 --> 01:45:01.755
Baby!

1891
01:45:01.922 --> 01:45:02.589
Torn!

1892
01:45:03.590 --> 01:45:04.257
Wait!

1893
01:45:39.835 --> 01:45:40.795
Slow down.

1894
01:45:40.836 --> 01:45:41.670
Slow down!

1895
01:45:41.837 --> 01:45:43.338
Bad trouble up ahead, sir!

1896
01:45:43.505 --> 01:45:46.466
Good. Wilson'll have
a little business at last.

1897
01:45:46.967 --> 01:45:47.968
Let's take a look.

1898
01:45:48.134 --> 01:45:49.678
Oh, must we?

1899
01:45:50.303 --> 01:45:51.638
Just a look.

1900
01:45:52.305 --> 01:45:53.139
Come on.

1901
01:45:58.520 --> 01:46:01.565
Move back! Clear the space here!

1902
01:46:02.399 --> 01:46:03.775
Have a little respect!

1903
01:46:03.942 --> 01:46:06.382
If you don't have a legitimate reason
to be in the building...

1904
01:46:06.528 --> 01:46:08.697
...I want you outside!

1905
01:46:14.661 --> 01:46:18.874
If you saw anything tonight you'd like
to talk to me about, well, then you can...

1906
01:46:19.040 --> 01:46:20.458
...talk to me outside.

1907
01:46:20.625 --> 01:46:22.502
I don't want you in here. Okay?

1908
01:46:25.171 --> 01:46:26.965
Please. Stand back. Please.

1909
01:46:27.132 --> 01:46:29.092
My God.

1910
01:46:31.094 --> 01:46:32.053
All right, folks.

1911
01:46:32.220 --> 01:46:35.056
Make some room, huh?
Make some room back there!

1912
01:46:35.307 --> 01:46:37.517
If you have no business, clear out!

1913
01:46:51.406 --> 01:46:52.126
Sir.

1914
01:46:52.240 --> 01:46:54.200
What are you doing? Sir!

1915
01:46:55.535 --> 01:46:56.415
Sir, I'm asking you...

1916
01:46:56.536 --> 01:46:57.579
Get off me!

1917
01:46:58.580 --> 01:47:00.040
I'm fine.

1918
01:47:02.000 --> 01:47:03.418
I'm fine.

1919
01:47:04.836 --> 01:47:06.129
You knew her, eh?

1920
01:47:13.303 --> 01:47:15.722
Not really. No.

1921
01:47:17.140 --> 01:47:19.060
I gotta ask you to step back, sir.

1922
01:47:22.687 --> 01:47:24.397
So, what happened?

1923
01:47:25.231 --> 01:47:26.942
She ran out in the road.

1924
01:47:27.525 --> 01:47:30.737
- Son of a bitch didn't even stop his car.
- Hey.

1925
01:47:30.904 --> 01:47:32.822
I saw it. It was a yellow car.

1926
01:47:33.281 --> 01:47:34.407
A big yellow car.

1927
01:47:34.574 --> 01:47:35.814
Damn thing came out of nowhere.

1928
01:47:35.909 --> 01:47:37.410
Yeah. Yeah, a big yellow...

1929
01:47:37.577 --> 01:47:39.621
...Duesy. Custom job.

1930
01:47:40.497 --> 01:47:43.416
You don't have to tell me
what kind of car it was.

1931
01:47:44.000 --> 01:47:46.169
I know what kind of car it was.

1932
01:47:47.087 --> 01:47:47.754
Wilson.

1933
01:47:47.921 --> 01:47:50.441
- Just pull yourself together!
- I know what kind of car it was!

1934
01:47:50.507 --> 01:47:51.925
He's upset! Everybody out!

1935
01:47:52.092 --> 01:47:54.177
Can't you see the man
needs peace and quiet?

1936
01:47:54.344 --> 01:47:56.888
Wilson! I just got here from New York.

1937
01:47:57.847 --> 01:47:59.265
No! Hey! Wilson!

1938
01:47:59.432 --> 01:48:01.810
I was bringing you the coupé.

1939
01:48:01.977 --> 01:48:03.436
Sit down! Sit down.

1940
01:48:03.603 --> 01:48:05.355
Get him a drink. Get him a drink.

1941
01:48:05.522 --> 01:48:06.815
That yellow car wasn't mine!

1942
01:48:06.982 --> 01:48:08.400
You hear? I haven't...

1943
01:48:08.566 --> 01:48:10.694
- ...seen it all afternoon.
- Hey!

1944
01:48:11.695 --> 01:48:12.696
What color's your car?

1945
01:48:13.822 --> 01:48:14.948
It's blue.

1946
01:48:15.115 --> 01:48:16.658
Coupé.

1947
01:48:16.825 --> 01:48:18.201
We just got here from New York.

1948
01:48:18.368 --> 01:48:19.494
They just stopped!

1949
01:48:26.668 --> 01:48:27.794
Okay.

1950
01:48:29.129 --> 01:48:30.380
Blue.

1951
01:48:33.633 --> 01:48:34.843
Right.

1952
01:48:35.010 --> 01:48:36.136
Here.

1953
01:48:37.137 --> 01:48:38.555
Leave us alone.

1954
01:48:39.889 --> 01:48:40.724
Wilson.

1955
01:48:45.145 --> 01:48:46.730
Who owns the yellow car?

1956
01:48:51.026 --> 01:48:52.861
A fella named Gatsby.

1957
01:48:53.236 --> 01:48:54.738
Jay Gatsby.

1958
01:48:56.156 --> 01:48:57.699
He's a crook, George.

1959
01:48:58.199 --> 01:49:01.244
Yes. Throws those parties
the papers are always talking about.

1960
01:49:07.834 --> 01:49:11.629
Maybe he was the one that was
fooling around with Myrtle.

1961
01:49:12.213 --> 01:49:12.922
Maybe...

1962
01:49:13.089 --> 01:49:14.799
...that's why he killed her.

1963
01:49:14.966 --> 01:49:17.427
Yeah, maybe.

1964
01:49:18.678 --> 01:49:19.721
Guy like that, who knows?

1965
01:49:22.974 --> 01:49:26.019
He didn't have to kill her.

1966
01:49:35.904 --> 01:49:37.197
Gatsby.

1967
01:49:38.990 --> 01:49:42.035
Something ought to be done
about a fella like that.

1968
01:49:44.913 --> 01:49:46.247
<i>He'll pay.</i>

1969
01:49:47.290 --> 01:49:49.042
<i>Oh, he'll pay.</i>

1970
01:49:55.673 --> 01:49:57.092
Coward.

1971
01:49:58.426 --> 01:50:00.470
He didn't even stop his car.

1972
01:50:02.722 --> 01:50:04.432
You gonna defend him now?

1973
01:50:05.308 --> 01:50:06.726
Huh?

1974
01:50:24.619 --> 01:50:26.287
I'll telephone for a taxi, Nick.

1975
01:50:26.454 --> 01:50:28.498
Why don't you come inside
and have some supper...

1976
01:50:28.665 --> 01:50:29.385
...while you wait?

1977
01:50:29.541 --> 01:50:31.459
No, thanks. I'll wait outside.

1978
01:50:33.670 --> 01:50:34.546
What is the matter...

1979
01:50:34.712 --> 01:50:35.797
...with you?

1980
01:50:40.802 --> 01:50:41.845
Won't you come in, Nick?

1981
01:50:42.011 --> 01:50:43.012
No, thanks.

1982
01:50:43.179 --> 01:50:44.059
It's only half past 9.

1983
01:50:44.222 --> 01:50:45.181
No, I've had enough.

1984
01:50:45.807 --> 01:50:46.933
Of everyone.

1985
01:51:03.032 --> 01:51:04.701
Hello, old sport.

1986
01:51:13.418 --> 01:51:14.836
What are you doing?

1987
01:51:16.462 --> 01:51:17.922
Just sitting here.

1988
01:51:18.089 --> 01:51:19.549
Yes, I see that.

1989
01:51:19.716 --> 01:51:21.509
You see any trouble out on the road?

1990
01:51:21.676 --> 01:51:22.427
That woman...

1991
01:51:22.594 --> 01:51:24.679
- ...you ran down is dead, Jay.
- I thought so.

1992
01:51:24.929 --> 01:51:25.847
I...

1993
01:51:26.014 --> 01:51:27.254
I told Daisy that I thought so.

1994
01:51:27.390 --> 01:51:29.350
Daisy? Do you hear yourself?

1995
01:51:29.517 --> 01:51:30.476
How could you?

1996
01:51:30.643 --> 01:51:31.561
How could you do that?

1997
01:51:31.728 --> 01:51:32.729
Keep your voice down.

1998
01:51:32.896 --> 01:51:33.856
You're nothing...

1999
01:51:33.980 --> 01:51:36.149
- ...but a goddamn coward!
- Keep your voice down!

2000
01:51:36.316 --> 01:51:38.651
Please. There was no point in stop...

2001
01:51:38.818 --> 01:51:42.071
No point? No point?
What about that woman?

2002
01:51:42.238 --> 01:51:44.616
No point. She was killed instantly.

2003
01:51:44.782 --> 01:51:46.662
Yes, it ripped her open!
I was there! I saw her!

2004
01:51:46.784 --> 01:51:49.829
I understand! I understand!
It was my fault.

2005
01:51:49.996 --> 01:51:51.247
It was my fault. This woman...

2006
01:51:51.414 --> 01:51:55.210
...she rushed out at us
as if she was trying to speak to us.

2007
01:51:55.376 --> 01:51:58.922
It all happened so quickly.
She tried... I tried...

2008
01:51:59.088 --> 01:52:00.248
- ...to turn in time...
- "She"?

2009
01:52:00.298 --> 01:52:01.507
...but...

2010
01:52:18.483 --> 01:52:19.692
It was Daisy.

2011
01:52:25.114 --> 01:52:26.699
Well, I...

2012
01:52:29.244 --> 01:52:33.539
You see, after we left New York,
she was very nervous.

2013
01:52:33.706 --> 01:52:37.001
She thought that driving would steady her.

2014
01:52:38.503 --> 01:52:41.130
But this woman, she rushed...

2015
01:52:41.297 --> 01:52:42.507
<i>...out at us.</i>

2016
01:52:44.801 --> 01:52:45.882
It all happened so quickly.

2017
01:52:47.679 --> 01:52:48.638
It wasn't her fault...

2018
01:52:48.805 --> 01:52:49.847
<i>...do you see?</i>

2019
01:53:11.411 --> 01:53:14.372
No one must know that Daisy was driving.

2020
01:53:15.707 --> 01:53:16.708
Promise me.

2021
01:53:18.376 --> 01:53:21.671
Jay, you should go home
and get some rest.

2022
01:53:21.838 --> 01:53:24.882
I'm going to wait here.
I'm going to wait here all night...

2023
01:53:25.049 --> 01:53:26.289
- ...if necessary.
- No, no, no.

2024
01:53:26.384 --> 01:53:27.802
- That's not a good idea.
- If he tries...

2025
01:53:27.969 --> 01:53:30.430
...to bother her about that
unpleasantness this afternoon...

2026
01:53:30.596 --> 01:53:31.717
If he tries to bother her...

2027
01:53:32.223 --> 01:53:34.684
If he tries any brutality
on her whatsoever...

2028
01:53:34.851 --> 01:53:37.020
Torn won't touch her.
He's not even thinking about her.

2029
01:53:37.186 --> 01:53:38.980
I don't trust him.

2030
01:53:39.147 --> 01:53:41.816
- I don't trust him.
- All right.

2031
01:53:42.275 --> 01:53:42.942
All right.

2032
01:53:46.487 --> 01:53:47.864
You wait here.

2033
01:53:49.365 --> 01:53:50.783
I'll see if there's any commotion.

2034
01:53:52.327 --> 01:53:53.453
Would you?

2035
01:53:58.291 --> 01:54:00.043
Thank you, old sport.

2036
01:54:06.507 --> 01:54:09.052
Listen, she was in the wrong,
running out on the road.

2037
01:54:09.844 --> 01:54:11.220
Sweetheart, you have...

2038
01:54:11.387 --> 01:54:12.764
...nothing to worry about.

2039
01:54:12.930 --> 01:54:15.183
Let me take care of things.

2040
01:54:15.350 --> 01:54:16.851
Take care of you.

2041
01:54:17.101 --> 01:54:18.644
I'll make some calls.

2042
01:54:18.811 --> 01:54:20.646
We'll go away.

2043
01:54:21.356 --> 01:54:23.691
Just go away, get out of town.

2044
01:54:24.317 --> 01:54:25.985
Get some rest. Don't worry.

2045
01:54:26.861 --> 01:54:28.905
It'll be all right.

2046
01:54:31.074 --> 01:54:32.408
Hey.

2047
01:55:00.061 --> 01:55:03.189
Oh. Hello, old sport.

2048
01:55:03.439 --> 01:55:04.440
Jay.

2049
01:55:05.233 --> 01:55:06.818
- Everything all right?
- Yes.

2050
01:55:07.568 --> 01:55:09.237
Everything's just fine.

2051
01:55:09.737 --> 01:55:12.990
Around 4:00, she came to the window.

2052
01:55:13.825 --> 01:55:15.493
She stood there, then...

2053
01:55:16.828 --> 01:55:18.996
Then she turned out the light, so...

2054
01:55:19.664 --> 01:55:20.873
Well...

2055
01:55:22.208 --> 01:55:24.252
Give me a hand, will you, old sport?

2056
01:55:24.961 --> 01:55:28.423
<i>I should have told him
what I had just seen, but...</i>

2057
01:55:29.382 --> 01:55:30.758
<i>...all I could manage was:</i>

2058
01:55:32.135 --> 01:55:34.095
You know, Jay,
with everything that's happened...

2059
01:55:34.262 --> 01:55:35.847
...you ought to go away.

2060
01:55:36.013 --> 01:55:37.432
Tonight. They'll trace your car.

2061
01:55:37.598 --> 01:55:39.517
Go away? No, I can't leave now.

2062
01:55:39.684 --> 01:55:40.685
Not tonight.

2063
01:55:40.852 --> 01:55:42.937
Jay, do you understand
that a woman has been killed?

2064
01:55:43.104 --> 01:55:44.772
Daisy's going to call in the morning.

2065
01:55:45.273 --> 01:55:48.109
Then we'll make plans
to go away together.

2066
01:55:50.153 --> 01:55:51.446
- Yeah.
- Daisy, she...

2067
01:55:51.612 --> 01:55:53.865
She just needs time
to think things through. Herzog...

2068
01:55:54.031 --> 01:55:55.867
...we're talking. Thank you.

2069
01:56:00.037 --> 01:56:01.956
She just needs time to think.

2070
01:56:02.123 --> 01:56:04.584
No, Jay. With Daisy...

2071
01:56:04.876 --> 01:56:09.714
She just needs time to think.
She's going to call in the morning.

2072
01:56:17.972 --> 01:56:18.639
Wait up with me.

2073
01:56:24.103 --> 01:56:25.146
The sun's almost up.

2074
01:56:29.650 --> 01:56:32.904
<i>That was the night
he finally told me the truth.</i>

2075
01:56:33.070 --> 01:56:34.322
<i>All of it.</i>

2076
01:56:37.325 --> 01:56:39.911
You know, I've thought for a while...

2077
01:56:40.077 --> 01:56:44.874
...I had a lot of things,
but the truth is I'm empty.

2078
01:56:45.041 --> 01:56:47.960
I suppose that's why
I make things up about myself.

2079
01:56:48.127 --> 01:56:50.922
But I've wanted to tell you
the whole story for...

2080
01:56:51.088 --> 01:56:52.507
...a very long time.

2081
01:56:53.674 --> 01:56:56.636
See, I grew up terribly, terribly poor,
old sport.

2082
01:56:56.844 --> 01:57:00.181
- My folks were...
<i>- He revealed his humble beginnings...</i>

2083
01:57:00.348 --> 01:57:03.559
<i>...his transformative voyage
with Dan Cody...</i>

2084
01:57:03.726 --> 01:57:06.354
<i>...the war, Oxford...</i>

2085
01:57:06.521 --> 01:57:09.815
<i>...and how he joined Wolfshiem
in the business.</i>

2086
01:57:11.234 --> 01:57:12.693
<i>It was also that night...</i>

2087
01:57:12.860 --> 01:57:16.948
<i>...that I became aware of Gatsby's
extraordinary gift for hope.</i>

2088
01:57:17.114 --> 01:57:20.159
I can't describe how surprised I was
to find out that I loved her.

2089
01:57:20.326 --> 01:57:21.452
And that she loved me too.

2090
01:57:21.619 --> 01:57:24.580
<i>A gift that I have never found
in any other person.</i>

2091
01:57:24.747 --> 01:57:27.333
I never realized how extraordinary...

2092
01:57:27.500 --> 01:57:29.418
...a nice girl could be.

2093
01:57:29.585 --> 01:57:33.130
<i>And which it is not likely
I shall ever find again.</i>

2094
01:57:33.297 --> 01:57:35.174
I thought out my life with Daisy in it...

2095
01:57:35.341 --> 01:57:38.861
...trying to figure out how we could marry and
struggle along on so many dollars a month.

2096
01:57:40.721 --> 01:57:42.473
What was in the letter?

2097
01:57:45.059 --> 01:57:49.647
The reason why after the war
I hadn't been able to return.

2098
01:57:50.314 --> 01:57:54.569
I asked her to wait until I made
something of myself, but she was...

2099
01:57:56.320 --> 01:57:58.614
See, I felt married to her.

2100
01:58:01.534 --> 01:58:02.868
That was all.

2101
01:58:03.786 --> 01:58:05.204
<i>It was all for her.</i>

2102
01:58:05.663 --> 01:58:07.873
<i>The house, the parties.</i>

2103
01:58:08.040 --> 01:58:09.208
<i>Everything.</i>

2104
01:58:16.716 --> 01:58:18.634
<i>God sees everything.</i>

2105
01:58:27.560 --> 01:58:29.520
Mr. Gatsby, sir, excuse me.

2106
01:58:29.937 --> 01:58:31.480
Chicago calling, sir.

2107
01:58:31.647 --> 01:58:32.898
Not now.

2108
01:58:33.566 --> 01:58:36.319
Keep it open for a personal call.

2109
01:58:36.902 --> 01:58:39.864
- A personal call? Of course.
- Excuse me, sir.

2110
01:58:40.031 --> 01:58:41.574
Mr. Gatsby, I'm gonna drain the pool.

2111
01:58:41.741 --> 01:58:43.618
Before the leaves start falling in.

2112
01:58:43.784 --> 01:58:45.244
Not today.

2113
01:58:47.872 --> 01:58:49.874
Not today, it's so beautiful.

2114
01:58:51.000 --> 01:58:56.339
You know, old sport, I haven't used that
pool once all summer. Let's take a swim.

2115
01:58:57.757 --> 01:58:59.634
Have the phone brought down to the pool.

2116
01:59:13.606 --> 01:59:15.358
I have to go, Jay.

2117
01:59:18.027 --> 01:59:19.445
I have to work.

2118
01:59:19.945 --> 01:59:21.364
I understand.

2119
01:59:23.074 --> 01:59:24.325
Well...

2120
01:59:24.492 --> 01:59:26.035
...I'll walk you out.

2121
01:59:33.709 --> 01:59:36.504
Well, I'll call you up.

2122
01:59:36.712 --> 01:59:39.173
Please do, old sport. Please do.

2123
01:59:42.009 --> 01:59:42.718
I suppose...

2124
01:59:42.885 --> 01:59:44.679
...that Daisy will call too.

2125
01:59:48.474 --> 01:59:49.934
I...

2126
01:59:51.018 --> 01:59:52.311
I suppose.

2127
01:59:53.437 --> 01:59:54.563
Yes.

2128
01:59:56.774 --> 01:59:58.693
Well, goodbye.

2129
02:00:17.545 --> 02:00:18.796
Jay!

2130
02:00:20.965 --> 02:00:22.508
They're a rotten crowd.

2131
02:00:24.593 --> 02:00:27.304
You're worth the whole
damn bunch put together.

2132
02:00:37.273 --> 02:00:38.354
<i>I was always glad...</i>

2133
02:00:38.482 --> 02:00:39.734
<i>...I said that.</i>

2134
02:00:41.986 --> 02:00:42.737
<i>It was the only...</i>

2135
02:00:42.903 --> 02:00:44.363
<i>...compliment I ever paid him.</i>

2136
02:00:48.617 --> 02:00:49.618
<i>That morning...</i>

2137
02:00:50.369 --> 02:00:52.663
<i>Wall Street boomed its usual...</i>

2138
02:00:52.830 --> 02:00:53.998
<i>golden roar.</i>

2139
02:00:54.165 --> 02:00:57.960
<i>But I wasn't worth a decent stroke of work.</i>

2140
02:00:58.878 --> 02:01:01.839
<i>I waited for Gatsby to call with news.</i>

2141
02:01:04.300 --> 02:01:05.968
<i>While he waited...</i>

2142
02:01:06.135 --> 02:01:07.470
<i>...for Daisy.</i>

2143
02:01:49.845 --> 02:01:51.347
Gatsby's.

2144
02:01:52.765 --> 02:01:55.976
I know Mr. Gatsby will be
very happy that you've called.

2145
02:02:18.415 --> 02:02:20.167
Daisy.

2146
02:02:41.522 --> 02:02:43.899
Hello! Hello!

2147
02:02:44.066 --> 02:02:46.569
<i>Hello! Is everything all right?</i>

2148
02:02:52.324 --> 02:02:55.077
Hello! Hello!

2149
02:02:55.244 --> 02:02:56.620
Hello!

2150
02:02:56.787 --> 02:02:58.831
<i>Hello! ls everything all right?</i>

2151
02:03:08.966 --> 02:03:13.178
<i>I remember the rest of that day
as an endless drill...</i>

2152
02:03:13.345 --> 02:03:17.975
<i>...of police and photographers
and tabloid reporters.</i>

2153
02:03:18.684 --> 02:03:20.936
<i>The headlines were a nightmare.</i>

2154
02:03:22.104 --> 02:03:24.148
<i>They pinned everything on Gatsby.</i>

2155
02:03:24.315 --> 02:03:28.694
<i>The affair with Myrtle.
The hit-and-run. Everything.</i>

2156
02:03:28.903 --> 02:03:30.905
<i>And there was nothing I could say...</i>

2157
02:03:31.071 --> 02:03:36.118
<i>...except the one unutterable fact
that none of it was true.</i>

2158
02:03:36.452 --> 02:03:39.914
Darling, Daddy's taking care
of his two favorite girls.

2159
02:03:40.080 --> 02:03:41.290
Where are we going?

2160
02:03:41.832 --> 02:03:43.792
- We're going on a holiday.
- We should go.

2161
02:03:43.959 --> 02:03:45.252
Just you...

2162
02:03:45.419 --> 02:03:47.004
...me and Daddy.

2163
02:03:49.340 --> 02:03:50.591
Buchanan residence.

2164
02:03:50.758 --> 02:03:52.198
<i>May I speak to Mrs. Buchanan?</i>

2165
02:03:52.635 --> 02:03:54.136
It's Mr. Carraway, her cousin.

2166
02:03:54.303 --> 02:03:54.984
<i>Madame...</i>

2167
02:03:55.137 --> 02:03:57.014
...is not available, Monsieur Carraway.

2168
02:03:57.473 --> 02:04:00.351
If you speak to her,
tell her the funeral's tomorrow.

2169
02:04:00.517 --> 02:04:02.478
I'm sorry, monsieur,
they have gone away.

2170
02:04:02.645 --> 02:04:03.479
<i>Gone away?</i>

2171
02:04:03.938 --> 02:04:05.481
Do you know when they'll be back?

2172
02:04:08.150 --> 02:04:09.026
No.

2173
02:04:09.193 --> 02:04:11.153
<i>They have already departed.</i>

2174
02:04:15.115 --> 02:04:19.370
Please. I know that she would
want to be there. She would...

2175
02:04:19.578 --> 02:04:21.455
If you would just get a message to her.

2176
02:04:21.622 --> 02:04:24.833
<i>- Let me talk to her, please.</i>
- You wish to contact her?

2177
02:04:25.626 --> 02:04:27.461
I have no further information, monsieur.

2178
02:04:27.836 --> 02:04:29.004
Please!

2179
02:04:31.757 --> 02:04:33.092
Hello?

2180
02:04:40.015 --> 02:04:41.225
Come on.

2181
02:04:41.976 --> 02:04:44.395
<i>They were careless people, Tom and Daisy.</i>

2182
02:04:46.105 --> 02:04:48.273
<i>They smashed up things and people...</i>

2183
02:04:48.440 --> 02:04:51.110
<i>...and then retreated back
into their money and their...</i>

2184
02:04:51.276 --> 02:04:52.987
<i>vast carelessness.</i>

2185
02:04:54.697 --> 02:04:55.948
Hey.

2186
02:04:56.115 --> 02:04:57.783
Hey! Get out of here!

2187
02:04:58.242 --> 02:04:59.493
Go on!

2188
02:04:59.660 --> 02:05:01.203
Get the hell out of here!

2189
02:05:14.925 --> 02:05:16.176
<i>I rang...</i>

2190
02:05:16.343 --> 02:05:17.928
<i>...I wrote...</i>

2191
02:05:18.095 --> 02:05:20.097
<i>...I implored.</i>

2192
02:05:20.681 --> 02:05:26.020
<i>But not a single one of the sparkling
hundreds that enjoyed his hospitality...</i>

2193
02:05:26.895 --> 02:05:29.398
<i>...attended the funeral.</i>

2194
02:05:29.857 --> 02:05:31.650
<i>And from Daisy...</i>

2195
02:05:32.359 --> 02:05:34.319
<i>...not even a flower.</i>

2196
02:05:36.030 --> 02:05:38.365
<i>I was all he had.</i>

2197
02:05:38.949 --> 02:05:41.368
<i>The only one who cared.</i>

2198
02:06:12.483 --> 02:06:14.568
<i>After Gatsby's death...</i>

2199
02:06:15.527 --> 02:06:17.988
<i>...New York was haunted for me.</i>

2200
02:06:19.156 --> 02:06:20.532
<i>That city...</i>

2201
02:06:22.493 --> 02:06:26.205
<i>...my once golden shimmering mirage...</i>

2202
02:06:27.664 --> 02:06:29.875
<i>...now made me sick.</i>

2203
02:06:33.504 --> 02:06:34.379
<i>On my last night...</i>

2204
02:06:34.546 --> 02:06:35.964
<i>...in New York...</i>

2205
02:06:36.965 --> 02:06:38.884
<i>...I returned to that huge...</i>

2206
02:06:39.051 --> 02:06:41.929
<i>...incoherent house once more.</i>

2207
02:06:42.096 --> 02:06:43.472
<i>Wolfshiem's associates...</i>

2208
02:06:43.639 --> 02:06:45.140
<i>...had cleaned it out.</i>

2209
02:06:45.307 --> 02:06:46.827
<i>He threw all those parties...</i>

2210
02:06:46.850 --> 02:06:48.560
<i>...hoping she'd wander in one night.</i>

2211
02:06:48.727 --> 02:06:50.127
<i>It's like an amusement park.</i>

2212
02:06:50.229 --> 02:06:52.147
<i>But how do you live here all alone?</i>

2213
02:06:52.314 --> 02:06:54.525
She makes it look so splendid...

2214
02:06:54.691 --> 02:06:55.891
...don't you think, old sport?

2215
02:06:56.026 --> 02:06:57.945
Music! And then we can...

2216
02:06:58.112 --> 02:06:59.738
...dance all night!

2217
02:07:01.532 --> 02:07:02.893
<i>Will you come, old sport?</i>

2218
02:07:02.991 --> 02:07:04.118
<i>We need you.</i>

2219
02:07:04.284 --> 02:07:06.411
I wish it could always be like this.

2220
02:07:06.578 --> 02:07:08.247
It Will be.

2221
02:07:10.040 --> 02:07:15.379
<i>I remembered how we had all come to
Gatsby's and guessed at his corruption...</i>

2222
02:07:16.505 --> 02:07:18.549
<i>...while he stood before</i> us...

2223
02:07:18.715 --> 02:07:19.383
<i>...concealing...</i>

2224
02:07:19.550 --> 02:07:22.094
<i>...an incorruptible dream.</i>

2225
02:07:23.095 --> 02:07:28.016
<i>It's perfect. From your perfect,
irresistible imagination.</i>

2226
02:07:34.106 --> 02:07:35.941
<i>The moon rose higher.</i>

2227
02:07:37.651 --> 02:07:40.863
<i>And as I stood there,
brooding on the old, unknown world...</i>

2228
02:07:41.905 --> 02:07:43.949
<i>...I thought of Gatsby's wonder...</i>

2229
02:07:44.116 --> 02:07:47.870
<i>...when he first picked out the green light
at the end of Daisy's dock.</i>

2230
02:07:53.458 --> 02:07:55.794
<i>He had come such a long way.</i>

2231
02:07:56.587 --> 02:07:59.047
<i>And his dream
must have seemed so close...</i>

2232
02:07:59.214 --> 02:08:02.176
<i>...that he could hardly fail to grasp it.</i>

2233
02:08:06.513 --> 02:08:07.639
<i>But he did not know...</i>

2234
02:08:07.806 --> 02:08:09.933
<i>...that it was already behind him.</i>

2235
02:08:11.768 --> 02:08:14.730
<i>Gatsby believed in the green light...</i>

2236
02:08:14.938 --> 02:08:18.317
<i>...the orgastic future
that year by year recedes...</i>

2237
02:08:18.483 --> 02:08:19.610
<i>...before us.</i>

2238
02:08:19.776 --> 02:08:22.529
<i>It eluded us then, but that's no matter.</i>

2239
02:08:22.905 --> 02:08:25.657
<i>Tomorrow, we will run faster...</i>

2240
02:08:25.824 --> 02:08:28.368
<i>...stretch out our arms farther...</i>

2241
02:08:28.827 --> 02:08:31.371
<i>...and one fine morning...</i>

2242
02:08:31.538 --> 02:08:33.624
<i>So we beat on...</i>

2243
02:08:34.082 --> 02:08:36.418
<i>...boats against the current...</i>

2244
02:08:37.002 --> 02:08:38.754
<i>...borne back...</i>

2245
02:08:38.921 --> 02:08:40.756
<i>...ceaselessly...</i>

2246
02:08:41.131 --> 02:08:43.175
<i>...into the past.</i>
